al YoutubeDL instance and monkeypatching its urlopen might be cleanest. Or create a fake ydl object with the needed methods: urlopen, to_screen, report_error, report_warning, etc.\n\nActually `self.to_screen` - FragmentFD has to_screen? Let me check. FragmentFD extends FileDownloader. FileDownloader has to_screen, report_error, report_warning, report_retry_fragment, report_skip_fragment... Let me check with get_info.\n\nLet me plan the fake ydl:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL:\n    def __init__(self, responses):\n        self.responses = responses  # dict url -> bytes or callable\n        self.params = {}\n    def urlopen(self, url):\n        ...\n    def to_screen(self, msg): pass\n    ...\n```\n\nActually, better to use the real `YoutubeDL` class with `params={'quiet': True}` and monkeypatch `urlopen`. But YoutubeDL's constructor might do stuff. It's usually fine: `YoutubeDL(params)` works.\n\nHmm, but HlsFD is instantiated as `HlsFD(ydl, params)`. FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params) sets self.ydl = ydl, self.params = params (or ydl.params?). Let me check FileDownloader.\n\nLet me get info on FileDownloader too.\n\nThe plan for tests:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.hls import HlsFD\nfrom youtube_dl import YoutubeDL\n```\n\nFor each test:\n- Create YoutubeDL with params.\n- Monkeypatch YoutubeDL.urlopen to return a fake response object with geturl() and read().\n- Monkeypatch HlsFD.can_download to return True (or craft manifest that can_download accepts).\n- Monkeypatch fragment methods: _prepare_and_start_frag_download, _download_fragment, _append_fragment, _finish_frag_download to record calls.\n- Call real_download and assert results.\n\nBut wait - the task says to execute lines 61-216, which includes can_download being called. If I monkeypatch can_download, line 68 executes but the real can_download isn't executed. That's fine since can_download is presumably outside lines 61-216 (it's a separate method). But to be safe, maybe use real manifests that can_download accepts. Let me check can_download's implementation.\n\nLet me recall youtube_dl's hls.py can_download:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE' ... \n        )\n```\n\nActually in youtube_dl (the fork), can_download is:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = [\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION(?:-METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)[A-Z0-9-]+|(?<!METHOD=)>)',\n            # r'#EXT-X-BYTERANGE',  # not supported by ffmpeg?\n            r'#EXT-X-I-FRAME-STREAM-INFO',\n        ]\n        if (info_dict.get('extra_param_to_segment_url') or\n                info_dict.get('_decryption_key_url')):\n            # pycrypto not found, skip unencrypted\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me get_info on can_download.\n\nActually in youtube-dl's hls.py:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE',\n            ...\n        )\n```\n\nI really need to check. Let me use get_info.\n\nAlso need to check `_prepare_url`:\n\n```python\n    def _prepare_url(self, info_dict, url):\n        \"\"\"Zero-some entries in URLs from info_dict.\n\n        ...\n        \"\"\"\n```\n\nHmm, in newer versions there's `_prepare_url` that handles URL query params from info_dict. Let me check.\n\nLet me also check FragmentFD methods:\n- `_prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` - calls `self._prepare_frag_download(ctx)` which uses `self._start_frag_download(ctx)`.\n- `_download_fragment(ctx, url, info_dict, headers)` - returns (success, content)\n- `_append_fragment(ctx, frag_content)`\n- `_finish_frag_download(ctx)`\n\nFor testing, I could monkeypatch these to avoid actual network/file operations. But then lines inside them aren't covered - but they're outside 61-216 anyway (they're in fragment.py). The requirement is lines 61-216 of hls.py.\n\nHowever, `_download_fragment` returns (success, frag_content) - I need to monkeypatch it to return True and content. And `_append_fragment` to collect content. And `_finish_frag_download` to do nothing (or return something). And `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` to set ctx['fragment_index'] = 0 maybe.\n\nWait, line 135: `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']: continue`. ctx['fragment_index'] is set by _prepare_and_start_frag_download presumably. If I monkeypatch it, I need to set ctx['fragment_index'] = 0 myself. Let me check what _prepare_and_start_frag_download does - it probably calls _prepare_frag_download which sets ctx = {..., 'fragment_index': 0, ...}.\n\nLet me check with get_info.\n\nAlso `report_retry_fragment` and `report_skip_fragment` - these are FileDownloader methods that use self.to_screen etc. If I use a real YoutubeDL, they should work fine.\n\nFor the HTTPError retry test, I need `_download_fragment` to raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError. HTTPError requires url, code, msg, hdrs, fp. fp can be None? Actually HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp) - fp can be None in some cases but read() would fail. We don't read it, so None might be OK. Actually `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` is urllib.error.HTTPError. Constructing with fp=None is fine as long as nothing reads it.\n\nHmm wait, actually in the retry loop, `self._download_fragment` raising HTTPError - but real _download_fragment catches errors internally? Let me check. In fragment.py:\n\n```python\n    def _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers, request_data=None):\n        success = False\n        ...\n        try:\n            ...\n        except ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in youtube_dl's fragment.py:\n\n```python\n    def _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers, request_data=None):\n        success = False\n        down = io.BytesIO()\n        ...\n        try:\n            ...\n            success = True\n        except ...\n```\n\nI don't remember. Let me check with get_info. Actually the code in hls.py catches HTTPError from _download_fragment, so _download_fragment must be able to raise it. Let me check.\n\nOK let me just call get_info on all the relevant names first.\n\nPlan of get_info calls (independent, can batch):\n- HlsFD\n- HlsFD.can_download\n- HlsFD._prepare_url\n- FragmentFD\n- FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download\n- FragmentFD._download_fragment\n- FragmentFD._append_fragment\n- FragmentFD._finish_frag_download\n- FileDownloader\n- FileDownloader.report_retry_fragment\n- FileDownloader.report_skip_fragment\n- FileDownloader.to_screen\n- FileDownloader.report_error\n- FileDownloader.report_warning\n- parse_m3u8_attributes\n- update_url_query\n\nThat's a lot; let me batch them.\n\nThen write tests.\n\nLet me think about the manifest content for can_download. From youtube-dl source (version ~2021.12.17):\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE',\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=(?!AES-128|NONE)[A-Z0-9-]+',\n            r'#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE=[1-9]',\n            r'#EXT-X-ALLOW-CACHE=YES(?!)',\n            ...\n        )\n```\n\nHmm, I really don't remember. Let me check the actual youtube_dl hls.py can_download:\n\nFrom youtube-dl master (youtube_dl/downloader/hls.py):\n\n```python\nclass HlsFD(FragmentFD):\n    \"\"\" Download HLS streams with the native downloader \"\"\"\n\n    FD_NAME = 'hlsnative'\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _extract_m3u8_...  # no\n\n    def _prepare_url(self, info_dict, url):\n        headers = info_dict.get('http_headers', {})\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me actually recall the real source:\n\n```python\nclass HlsFD(FragmentFD):\n    \"\"\" Download HLS streams with the native downloader \"\"\"\n\n    FD_NAME = 'hlsnative'\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE',\n            # https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-pantos-http-live-streaming-18#section-4.3.2.4\n            r'#EXT-X-BYTERANGE',\n            # https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-pantos-http-live-streaming-18#section-4.3.2.2\n            r'#EXT-X-I-FRAMES-ONLY',\n            ...\n        )\n```\n\nI can't recall precisely. The get_info tool will tell me. Let me just call it.\n\nActually, I recall the youtube-dl hls.py can_download:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION(?:-METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)[A-Z0-9-]+|(?<!METHOD=)>)',\n            # 1. #EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE\n            #    is not supported since it's not implemented by ffmpeg\n            # 2. #EXT-X-ENCRYPTION with a key format other than\n            #    'identity' is not supported\n            r'#EXT-X-BYTERANGE',\n            # playlists not defined by byterange are not supported\n            r'#EXT-X-I-FRAMES-ONLY',\n            ...\n        )\n```\n\nHmm no. OK, get_info it is.\n\nActually, I remember now - youtube-dl's hls.py:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE',\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=(?!AES-128|NONE)[A-Z0-9-]+',\n            r'#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)[A-Z0-9-]+',\n            r'#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(NONE|AES-128)[,][^,]*URI=\"[^\"]*\"[^,]*,IV',\n            ...\n        )\n```\n\nNo. Stop guessing; use the tool.\n\nLet me also think about what `can_download` checks regarding `extra_param_to_segment_url`:\n\nIn youtube-dl:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            ...\n        )\n        return all(not re.search(feature, manifest) for feature in UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES)\n```\n\nAnd in real_download:\n\n```python\n        if not self.can_download(s, info_dict):\n            if info_dict.get('extra_param_to_segment_url') or info_dict.get('_decryption_key_url'):\n                self.report_error('pycrypto not found. Please install it.')\n                return False\n            self.report_warning(...)\n```\n\nHmm wait, that doesn't quite make sense... Actually it does: if the manifest has unsupported features AND pycrypto is not installed... no wait.\n\nActually the real code:\n\n```python\n    def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n        man_url = info_dict['url']\n        self.to_screen('[%s] Downloading m3u8 manifest' % self.FD_NAME)\n\n        urlh = self.ydl.urlopen(self._prepare_url(info_dict, man_url))\n        man_url = urlh.geturl()\n        s = urlh.read().decode('utf-8', 'ignore')\n\n        if not self.can_download(s, info_dict):\n            if info_dict.get('extra_param_to_segment_url') or info_dict.get('_decryption_key_url'):\n                self.report_error('pycrypto not found. Please install it.')\n                return False\n            self.report_warning(\n                'hlsnative has detected features it does not support, '\n                'extraction will be delegated to ffmpeg')\n            fd = FFmpegFD(self.ydl, self.params)\n            for ph in self._progress_hooks:\n                fd.add_progress_hook(ph)\n            return fd.real_download(filename, info_dict)\n```\n\nHmm, wait. That's odd - can_download checks for pycrypto availability? Let me think... Actually yes! In youtube-dl, can_download includes:\n\n```python\n        if not is_installed('pycrypto')... \n```\n\nNo... Actually I recall:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE',\n            ...\n        )\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, the logic: if the manifest contains AES-128 encryption and pycrypto is not available, can_download returns False. Then in real_download, if extra_param_to_segment_url or _decryption_key_url is set, it means the user explicitly wants native download (those params are only used by native), so report error about pycrypto. Otherwise delegate to ffmpeg.\n\nSo can_download probably has something like:\n\n```python\n        if not can_decrypt... \n```\n\nHmm, actually I remember now! The real youtube-dl code:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def can_download(manifest, info_dict):\n        UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = (\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=NONE',\n            r'#EXT-X-ENCRYPTION-METHOD=(?!AES-128|NONE)[A-Z0-9-]+',\n            r'#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)[A-Z0-9-]+',\n            r'#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(NONE|AES-128)[,][^,]*URI=\"[^\"]*\"[^,]*,IV',\n            ...\n        )\n```\n\nNo, stop. Let me just use the tool. I'll batch the get_info calls.\n\nActually, I realize the get_info tool \"Returns information about a symbol\" - probably it returns the source code of the symbol. Let me call it for the key symbols.\n\nLet me batch:\n1. HlsFD.can_download\n2. HlsFD._prepare_url\n3. FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download\n4. FragmentFD._download_fragment\n5. FragmentFD._append_fragment\n6. FragmentFD._finish_frag_download\n7. FileDownloader.report_retry_fragment\n8. FileDownloader.report_skip_fragment\n9. HlsFD (class info)\n10. FragmentFD (class info)\n\nThen based on results, write tests.\n\nLet me now think about the test structure more concretely.\n\n```python\nimport io\nimport re\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.hls import HlsFD\nfrom youtube_dl import YoutubeDL\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    def __init__(self, url, data):\n        self._url = url\n        self._data = data\n\n    def geturl(self):\n        return self._url\n\n    def read(self):\n        return self._data\n```\n\nFor the ydl, I'll use a real YoutubeDL with params={'quiet': True} and monkeypatch its urlopen. Actually, HlsFD uses self.ydl.urlopen. If I create YoutubeDL(params) and monkeypatch instance method: `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)`. That works.\n\nBut wait - FileDownloader methods like to_screen use self.ydl.to_screen? Let me check FileDownloader.to_screen:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, msg, skip_eol=False):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(self.__msg(msg), skip_eol=skip_eol)\n```\n\nYes, delegates to ydl. report_error -> self.ydl.report_error. report_warning -> self.ydl.report_warning. So a real YoutubeDL with quiet=True handles all that.\n\nNow, the FFmpegFD delegation test: FFmpegFD(self.ydl, self.params) is constructed inside real_download, then fd.real_download is called. I need to monkeypatch FFmpegFD in the hls module namespace: `monkeypatch.setattr('youtube_dl.downloader.hls.FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)`. The fake's real_download records the call and returns True. Also `fd.add_progress_hook(ph)` must exist.\n\nNote: `self._progress_hooks` - FileDownloader has _progress_hooks list, and add_progress_hook. Fine.\n\nNow for the fragment methods. Let me monkeypatch on the HlsFD instance:\n\n```python\ndef fake_prepare_and_start(self, ctx):\n    ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n```\n\nWait, but _prepare_and_start_frag_download is called as self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx). If I monkeypatch the class method or instance? Since I create the instance in the test, I can just assign instance attributes:\n\n```python\ndl = HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params)\ncalls = []\ndl._prepare_and_start_frag_download = lambda ctx: (ctx.update({'fragment_index': 0}), calls.append(('start', ctx)))[1]\n```\n\nHmm, but wait - does _prepare_and_start_frag_download set ctx['fragment_index']? Let me check FragmentFD._prepare_frag_download:\n\n```python\n    def _prepare_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        if 'live' not in ctx:\n            ctx['live'] = False\n        if not ctx['live'] and not (self.params.ge