        "reasoning_content": "Let me now write the complete test file based on what I know.\n\nKey facts gathered:\n- `HlsFD.can_download(manifest, info_dict)`:\n  - UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = ('#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)', '#EXT-X-MAP:')\n  - is_aes128_enc = '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128' in manifest\n  - checks: no unsupported features, can_decrypt_frag or not aes128, not (aes128 and BYTERANGE), not info_dict.get('is_live')\n- `FragmentFD._prepare_url(info_dict, url)`: returns sanitized_Request(url, None, headers) if info_dict has http_headers else url.\n- `FragmentFD._download_fragment(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None)` returns (success, content).\n- `FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` calls _prepare_frag_download + _start_frag_download.\n- `FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, retries)` uses to_screen, error_to_compat_str, format_retries.\n- `FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment(frag_index)` uses to_screen.\n- `can_decrypt_frag = True` (module-level in hls.py presumably).\n- `parse_m3u8_attributes` parses KEY=value pairs.\n- FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params): self.ydl = ydl, self._progress_hooks = [], self.params = params, add_progress_hook(self.report_progress).\n\nImportant: FileDownloader.__init__ calls self.add_progress_hook(self.report_progress). So _progress_hooks contains report_progress. In the FFmpeg delegation path, `for ph in self._progress_hooks: fd.add_progress_hook(ph)` \u2014 my fake FFmpegFD must accept hooks. Fine.\n\nNow, since can_decrypt_frag is True (Crypto installed), AES-128 manifests pass can_download (as long as no BYTERANGE with AES-128, no unsupported methods, not live).\n\nManifests that pass can_download:\n- Plain: `#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n` \u2014 no #EXT-X-KEY, no #EXT-X-MAP \u2192 passes.\n- AES-128: `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"...\"` \u2192 passes (can_decrypt_frag True), but must NOT contain #EXT-X-BYTERANGE.\n- Unsupported: `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=SAMPLE-AES` or `#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"` \u2192 fails can_download.\n\nNow write the tests. Structure:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Coverage tests for youtube_dl.downloader.hls.HlsFD.real_download (lines 61-216).\"\"\"\n\nimport binascii\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl import YoutubeDL\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error, compat_struct_pack\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader import hls as hls_module\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.hls import HlsFD\n\nMANIFEST_URL = 'https://example.com/video.m3u8'\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    def __init__(self, url, data):\n        self._url = url\n        self._data = data\n\n    def geturl(self):\n        return self._url\n\n    def read(self):\n        return self._data\n\n\ndef _url_string(url):\n    if hasattr(url, 'get_full_url'):\n        return url.get_full_url()\n    return url\n\n\ndef make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, responses=None, params=None):\n    \"\"\"Build an HlsFD whose ydl.urlopen serves *manifest* (and extra responses).\"\"\"\n    ydl = YoutubeDL(params if params is not None else {'quiet': True})\n    responses = dict(responses or {})\n    requested = []\n\n    def fake_urlopen(url):\n        url = _url_string(url)\n        requested.append(url)\n        if url in responses:\n            result = responses[url]\n        else:\n            result = manifest\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return FakeResponse(url, result)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)\n    return HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params), requested\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)` \u2014 ydl is a local object; setattr on instance works with monkeypatch (it records and undoes, fine).\n\nHmm, one issue: `YoutubeDL(params)` \u2014 the real YoutubeDL __init__ with {'quiet': True}. It should be fine. But it may print stuff? With quiet=True, to_screen is suppressed. report_error raises YoutubeDLError? Let me think: YoutubeDL.report_error:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        self.trouble(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nand trouble:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        if self.params.get('logger'):\n            ...\n        else:\n            self.to_stderr(message)\n        if not expected:\n            self.params.get('logger')...\n        if self.params.get('raise_errors'...):\n            raise YoutubeDLError(message)\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        \"\"\"Report an error or warning...\"\"\"\n        if message:\n            if self.params.get('logger'):\n                self.params['logger'].error(message)\n            else:\n                self.to_stderr(message)\n        if self.params.get('raise_errors'):\n            raise YoutubeDLError(message)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure about raise_errors default. In youtube-dl, `raise_errors` defaults to False, so report_error just prints to stderr and sets... Actually there's also `self._download_retcode = 1` somewhere? In trouble:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        if message:\n            ...\n        if not expected:\n            self._download_retcode = 1\n```\n\nHmm, something like that. Either way, it won't raise by default. To be safe and to *assert* the error was reported, I'll monkeypatch `ydl.report_error` to record. Actually, better: monkeypatch the downloader's report_error? The HlsFD calls `self.report_error(...)` which is FileDownloader.report_error \u2192 `self.ydl.report_error(...)`. I can monkeypatch `dl.report_error` directly on the instance to record calls. That's cleaner for assertions and avoids stderr noise.\n\nSimilarly `report_warning` \u2192 `self.ydl.report_warning`. I'll monkeypatch `dl.report_warning` to record.\n\nBut careful: FileDownloader.report_error signature: `def report_error(self, *args, **kargs)` delegating. If I replace instance attribute `dl.report_error` with a lambda taking *args, fine.\n\nNow the fragment-method patcher:\n\n```python\ndef patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, results, fragment_index=0):\n    \"\"\"Replace FragmentFD's fragment machinery with recording fakes.\n\n    *results* is a list of ``(success, content)`` tuples or Exception\n    instances; the last entry is repeated once the list is exhausted.\n    \"\"\"\n    calls = {'prepare': [], 'download': [], 'append': [], 'finish': []}\n    state = {'next': 0}\n\n    def fake_prepare_and_start(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n        calls['prepare'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None):\n        calls['download'].append({'url': frag_url, 'headers': dict(headers or {})})\n        idx = state['next']\n        state['next'] += 1\n        result = results[idx] if idx < len(results) else results[-1]\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return result\n\n    def fake_append(ctx, frag_content):\n        calls['append'].append(frag_content)\n\n    def fake_finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_append_fragment', fake_append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_finish_frag_download', fake_finish)\n    return calls\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `results[-1]` fails if results is empty. Guard: if not results, default (True, b''). I'll ensure tests always pass non-empty results.\n\nHmm, also `dict(headers or {})` \u2014 headers may be a dict; fine.\n\nAES patcher:\n\n```python\ndef patch_aes(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Replace the module-level AES with a recording fake.\"\"\"\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeCipher(object):\n        def __init__(self, key, mode, iv):\n            self.key = key\n            self.mode = mode\n            self.iv = iv\n            calls.append(self)\n\n        def decrypt(self, data):\n            return b'decrypted:' + data\n\n    class FakeAES(object):\n        MODE_CBC = 'MODE_CBC'\n\n        @staticmethod\n        def new(key, mode, iv):\n            return FakeCipher(key, mode, iv)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)\n    return calls\n```\n\nFFmpeg patcher:\n\n```python\ndef patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch):\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeFFmpegFD(object):\n        def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n            self.ydl = ydl\n            self.params = params\n            self.hooks = []\n\n        def add_progress_hook(self, ph):\n            self.hooks.append(ph)\n\n        def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n            calls.append({'filename': filename, 'info_dict': info_dict, 'hooks': list(self.hooks)})\n            return True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)\n    return calls\n```\n\nNow the tests:\n\n### 1. test_delegates_to_ffmpeg_when_features_unsupported\n\nManifest with `#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"` (unsupported). No extra params. Expect:\n- returns True (from fake ffmpeg)\n- warning reported\n- ffmpeg called with filename and info_dict\n- progress hooks forwarded (FileDownloader.__init__ added report_progress hook, so hooks list non-empty)\n\n```python\ndef test_delegates_to_ffmpeg_when_features_unsupported(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    warnings = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_warning', lambda *a, **k: warnings.append(a))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL]\n    assert len(warnings) == 1\n    assert 'ffmpeg' in warnings[0][0]\n    assert len(ffmpeg_calls) == 1\n    assert ffmpeg_calls[0]['filename'] == 'out.ts'\n    assert ffmpeg_calls[0]['info_dict']['url'] == MANIFEST_URL\n    assert len(ffmpeg_calls[0]['hooks']) == len(dl._progress_hooks)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `dl._progress_hooks` contains report_progress (added in __init__). The fake fd's hooks should have the same count. Good.\n\nHmm, but `dl.report_warning` \u2014 FileDownloader.report_warning(self, *args, **kargs) \u2192 self.ydl.report_warning. Replacing instance attr with lambda *a: fine.\n\nAlso note: `man_url = urlh.geturl()` \u2014 FakeResponse.geturl returns the url string passed. In make_downloader, FakeResponse(url, result) where url is the string form. So man_url = MANIFEST_URL. Good.\n\n### 2. test_reports_pycrypto_error_with_extra_param\n\nManifest unsupported + info_dict has extra_param_to_segment_url. Expect return False, error reported.\n\n```python\ndef test_reports_pycrypto_error_with_extra_param(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', lambda *a, **k: errors.append(a))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(errors) == 1\n    assert 'pycrypto' in errors[0][0]\n    assert ffmpeg_calls == []\n```\n\n### 3. test_reports_pycrypto_error_with_decryption_key_url\n\nSame but `_decryption_key_url`.\n\n### 4. test_basic_download_all_fragments\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-TARGETDURATION:10\n#EXTINF:10,\nseg0.ts\n#EXTINF:10,\nhttps://other.example.com/seg1.ts\n```\ninfo_dict: {'url': MANIFEST_URL}. params default. results: [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')].\n\nExpect:\n- result True\n- downloads: first url = urljoin('https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'seg0.ts') = 'https://example.com/seg0.ts'; second = 'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts'\n- headers empty dicts\n- appended contents in order\n- finish called once\n- prepare called once with total_frags=2, ad_frags=0\n\n```python\ndef test_basic_download_all_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-TARGETDURATION:10\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL]\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['ad_frags'] == 0\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts', 'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert all(d['headers'] == {} for d in calls['download'])\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0', b'frag1']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 urljoin('https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'seg0.ts') = 'https://example.com/seg0.ts'. Yes.\n\n### 5. test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment\n\nparams {'quiet': True, 'test': True}. Manifest with 2 fragments. results [(True, b'frag0')].\n\nExpect: result True, one download, one append, no finish? Wait \u2014 `if test: break` happens after _append_fragment, then loop ends, then `self._finish_frag_download(ctx)` is called (line 214) \u2014 yes, finish IS called after break. So finish called once. And only 1 download/append.\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params={'quiet': True, 'test': True})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 1\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\n### 6. test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments\n\nfragment_index=1, manifest with 2 fragments. Expect only seg1 downloaded (frag_index 2 > 1). Download url 'https://example.com/seg1.ts'.\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag1')], fragment_index=1)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == ['https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag1']\n```\n\n### 7. test_extra_param_to_segment_url_added_to_fragment_urls\n\ninfo_dict with extra_param_to_segment_url='token=abc&sig=xyz'. Manifest with 1 fragment 'seg0.ts'. update_url_query('https://example.com/seg0.ts', {'token': ['abc'], 'sig': ['xyz']}) \u2192 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc&sig=xyz'.\n\n```python\ndef test_extra_param_to_segment_url_added_to_fragment_urls(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc&sig=xyz',\n    })\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['download'][0]['url'] == 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc&sig=xyz'\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 update_url_query ordering: parse_qs preserves order in py3.7+ (dict ordered). 'token=abc&sig=xyz' \u2192 {'token': ['abc'], 'sig': ['xyz']} \u2192 query 'token=abc&sig=xyz'. Should be fine. To be safe, use a single param: 'token=abc' \u2192 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc'. Safer. I'll use one param.\n\n### 8. test_ad_fragments_are_skipped\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=ad\nseg-ad0.ts\n#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=master\nseg0.ts\n#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad\nseg-ad1.ts\n#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment\nseg1.ts\n```\n\nWait \u2014 is_ad_fragment_start: `s.startswith('#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO') and 'type=ad' in s or s.startswith('#UPLYNK-SEGMENT') and s.endswith(',ad')`. Note operator precedence: (A and B) or (C and D). '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad' startswith '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT' and endswith ',ad' \u2192 True. Good.\n\nis_ad_fragment_end: '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO' and 'type=master' \u2192 True; '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment' \u2192 endswith ',segment' \u2192 True.\n\nSo counting loop: media_frags = 2 (seg0, seg1), ad_frags = 2. Download loop: ad fragments skipped via `if ad_frag_next: continue` (line 132-133), media fragments downloaded.\n\nExpect: prepare total_frags=2, ad_frags=2; downloads = [seg0, seg1] urls.\n\n```python\ndef test_ad_fragments_are_skipped(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=ad\\n'\n        'seg-ad0.ts\\n'\n        '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=master\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad\\n'\n        'seg-ad1.ts\\n'\n        '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['ad_frags'] == 2\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts', 'https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0', b'frag1']\n```\n\nWait \u2014 can_download on this manifest: no #EXT-X-KEY, no #EXT-X-MAP \u2192 passes. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad' \u2014 in the counting loop, is_ad_fragment_start('#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad') \u2192 True \u2192 ad_frag_next=True. Then 'seg-ad1.ts' \u2192 ad_frags += 1. Then '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment' \u2192 is_ad_fragment_start? startswith('#UPLYNK-SEGMENT') and endswith(',ad')? ',segment' doesn't end with ',ad' \u2192 False. is_ad_fragment_end \u2192 endswith(',segment') \u2192 True \u2192 ad_frag_next=False. Then 'seg1.ts' \u2192 media_frags += 1. \n\nIn the download loop: same logic with lines 209-212. Good.\n\n### 9. test_byterange_headers\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:1000@2000\nseg0.ts\n#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:500\nseg1.ts\n```\n\nFirst: sub_range_start=2000, byte_range={'start':2000,'end':3000}. Fragment 1: headers Range 'bytes=2000-2999'. Second: splitted_byte_range=['500'], len==1 \u2192 sub_range_start = byte_range['end'] = 3000; byte_range={'start':3000,'end':3500}. Fragment 2: Range 'bytes=3000-3499'.\n\ninfo_dict needs http_headers? Line 144: `headers = info_dict.get('http_headers', {})`. If absent, fresh {} each time, then Range added. Fine.\n\nExpect downloads with headers Range as above.\n\n```python\ndef test_byterange_sets_range_headers(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:1000@2000\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:500\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [d['headers'].get('Range') for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'bytes=2000-2999', 'bytes=3000-3499']\n```\n\n### 10. test_http_error_retries_then_skips\n\nparams: {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': True}. Manifest with 2 fragments. results: [HTTPError, HTTPError, (True, b'frag1')] \u2014 first fragment: attempt 0 raises (count\u21921), attempt 1 raises (count\u21922 > 1) \u2192 skip. Second fragment: (True, b'frag1').\n\nExpect: result True; downloads: seg0 twice, seg1 once; append only frag1.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retries_then_skips_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    params = {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': True}\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params=params)\n    error = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(MANIFEST_URL, 503, 'Service Unavailable', {}, None)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [error, error, (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts', 'https://example.com/seg0.ts',\n        'https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag1']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp): hdrs={} \u2014 in py3, HTTPError headers arg... `urllib.error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)` \u2014 hdrs should be an email.message or dict; dict works (it's passed to addinfourl... actually HTTPError.__init__ stores self.headers = hdrs). With fp=None, fine as long as nothing reads. report_retry_fragment does error_to_compat_str(err) \u2192 str(err) \u2192 'HTTP Error 503: Service Unavailable'. Fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` \u2014 in youtube_dl.compat, compat_urllib_error is the module urllib.error (py3). So HTTPError exists. Good.\n\n### 11. test_http_error_gives_up_when_skip_disabled\n\nparams: {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}. results: [error, error]. Expect result False, 2 downloads, no append, no finish.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_gives_up_when_skip_disabled(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    params = {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params=params)\n    error = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(MANIFEST_URL, 500, 'Server Error', {}, None)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [error, error])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 2\n    assert calls['append'] == []\n    assert calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\n### 12. test_fragment_download_failure_aborts\n\nresults: [(False, None)]. Expect result False, no append, no finish.\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_download_failure_aborts(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(False, None)])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 1\n    assert calls['append'] == []\n    assert calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\n### 13. test_aes128_decryption_with_iv\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0X1234567890ABCDEF1234567890ABCDEF\nseg0.ts\n```\n\nWait \u2014 IV=0x... : `binascii.unhexlify(decrypt_info['IV'][2:].zfill(32))`. IV value '0X1234...' \u2192 [2:] = '1234...'. zfill(32) \u2014 the value is already 32 chars \u2192 unhexlify gives 16 bytes. Good. Use lowercase '0x' to be conventional.\n\nKey fetch: ydl.urlopen(_prepare_url(info_dict, 'https://example.com/key.bin')).read() \u2192 responses['https://example.com/key.bin'] = b'0123456789abcdef' (16 bytes).\n\nresults: [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)] \u2014 content 16 bytes (multiple of block size) \u2014 but I'm patching AES anyway, so content can be anything. Still, patch AES to assert key/iv.\n\nExpect:\n- result True\n- AES called once with key=b'0123456789abcdef', mode=FakeAES.MODE_CBC, iv=unhexlify('1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef')\n- append gets b'decrypted:' + content\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_decryption_with_iv(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    key = b'0123456789abcdef'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': key})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL, 'https://example.com/key.bin']\n    assert len(aes_calls) == 1\n    assert aes_calls[0].key == key\n    assert aes_calls[0].iv == binascii.unhexlify('1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef')\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00' * 16]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 requested: first urlopen for manifest, then key fetch. But note: the key fetch happens inside the fragment loop at line 173-174, i.e., during processing of seg0.ts. Order: manifest fetch, then key fetch. Yes.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, `_prepare_url(info_dict, 'https://example.com/key.bin')` \u2014 info_dict has no http_headers \u2192 returns the url string. Good.\n\n### 14. test_aes128_default_iv_uses_media_sequence\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"key.bin\"\nseg0.ts\n```\n\n- MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5 \u2192 media_sequence = 5 (covers line 200-201)\n- KEY URI relative 'key.bin' \u2192 urljoin(man_url, 'key.bin') = 'https://example.com/key.bin' (covers 193-195)\n- No IV \u2192 iv = struct_pack('>8xq', 5) (covers 172)\n\nExpected iv: b'\\x00'*8 + struct.pack('>q', 5).\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_default_iv_uses_media_sequence_and_relative_key_uri(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert 'https://example.com/key.bin' in requested\n    assert aes_calls[0].iv == compat_struct_pack('>8xq', 5)\n    assert aes_calls[0].key == b'K' * 16\n```\n\nWait \u2014 compat_struct_pack \u2014 is it exported from youtube_dl.compat? The snippet imports `from youtube_dl.compat import ... compat_struct_pack`. Yes. compat_struct_pack = struct.pack.\n\nAlso note: media_sequence increments after each fragment: starts 5, after seg0 \u2192 6. Only one fragment, fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: `#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5` \u2014 line[22:] \u2014 '#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE' is 21 chars (let me count: #EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE = 1+4+1+1+5+1+8 = '#','E','X','T','-','X','-','M','E','D','I','A','-','S','E','Q','U','E','N','C','E' \u2192 21 chars). line[22:] = '5' (index 21 is ':'). Good.\n\n### 15. test_aes128_key_uri_updated_with_extra_query\n\ninfo_dict extra_param_to_segment_url='token=abc'. Manifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\nseg0.ts\n```\nKey URL after update: 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc'. responses must include that URL.\n\nExpect requested contains 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc'.\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_uri_gets_extra_query(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc' in requested\n```\n\n### 16. test_aes128_key_change_resets_cached_key\n\nManifest with two EXT-X-KEY lines with different URIs and two fragments:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\nseg0.ts\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key2.bin\"\nseg1.ts\n```\n\nFlow: line EXT-X-KEY1: decrypt_url=None, new URI key1 \u2192 KEY=None (line 198-199 covered). Fragment seg0: KEY None \u2192 fetch key1. Line EXT-X-KEY2: decrypt_url='https://example.com/key1.bin' != 'https://example.com/key2.bin' \u2192 KEY=None (covered again). Fragment seg1: fetch key2.\n\nresponses: key1.bin \u2192 b'1'*16, key2.bin \u2192 b'2'*16.\n\nExpect: aes_calls[0].key == b'1'*16, aes_calls[1].key == b'2'*16. requested includes both key URLs.\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_change_resets_cached_key(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key2.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={\n            'https://example.com/key1.bin': b'1' * 16,\n            'https://example.com/key2.bin': b'2' * 16,\n        })\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16), (True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [c.key for c in aes_calls] == [b'1' * 16, b'2' * 16]\n    assert 'https://example.com/key1.bin' in requested\n    assert 'https://example.com/key2.bin' in requested\n```\n\n### 17. test_aes128_with_byterange_delegates_to_ffmpeg\n\ncan_download: `not (is_aes128_enc and '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE' in manifest)` \u2192 False when both. So manifest with AES-128 KEY + BYTERANGE \u2192 can_download False \u2192 no extra params \u2192 ffmpeg delegation. This covers the can_download interplay but lines are same as test 1. Optional; adds branch coverage of can_download but not of 61-216 beyond test 1. I could include it for robustness. Hmm \u2014 it does exercise line 68 with a different reason. I'll include it as it's cheap.\n\nActually, let me reconsider \u2014 the task says lines 61-216 don't execute; my tests cover them. Extra tests are fine but let me not bloat too much. I'll include it since it verifies a distinct branch of can_download interaction... Actually can_download is a static method outside 61-216. The delegation path (69-78) is already covered by tests 1-3. I'll skip test 17 to keep the file focused. Hmm, but it's a nice sanity check. I'll skip it \u2014 keep the suite tight.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider one more time whether I should also cover `is_live` info_dict \u2192 can_download False \u2192 delegation. Also outside 61-216. Skip.\n\n### Blank lines / comment lines coverage\n\nLines 93-94 (blank line continue), 95-100 (comment line, not ad marker \u2192 continue). My manifests have '#EXTM3U' (comment line \u2192 line 95 \u2192 not ad start/end \u2192 continue at 100) and trailing newline producing an empty last line? `'#EXTM3U\\n...\\nseg1.ts\\n'.splitlines()` \u2192 last element 'seg1.ts', no trailing empty. Let me add explicit blank lines in one manifest to cover 93-94. E.g., in test 4 manifest, add '\\n' lines. Actually splitlines on 'a\\n\\nb' \u2192 ['a', '', 'b'] \u2192 empty line \u2192 continue (93-94). Let me add a blank line in the basic manifest. I'll add one blank line in test_basic manifest.\n\nAlso line 130 `if line:` \u2014 the else branch (empty line) \u2192 falls through to next iteration... wait, line 129-130: `line = line.strip(); if line:` \u2014 if empty, nothing happens (no continue needed since rest of loop body is inside if). Covered by blank line too.\n\n### Now, double-check the counting loop vs download loop for '#EXTM3U'\n\n'#EXTM3U' startswith '#' \u2192 is_ad_fragment_start? No. is_ad_fragment_end? No. \u2192 continue. Good.\n\n### Check `update_url_query` import in hls module \u2014 used at line 142, 197. Fine.\n\n### One more check: test 7 (extra query) \u2014 can_download passes (no KEY/MAP). Good. And line 120-121 covered.\n\n### Check test 13 (AES with IV): can_download \u2014 is_aes128_enc True, can_decrypt_frag True \u2192 OK; no BYTERANGE \u2192 OK; is_live not set \u2192 OK. Passes. Good.\n\n### Potential issue: `YoutubeDL({'quiet': True})` \u2014 does YoutubeDL __init__ require anything? It sets up logger etc. Should be fine. But it might create no files. OK.\n\nActually, hmm, one concern: `YoutubeDL(params)` \u2014 in youtube-dl, `YoutubeDL.__init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True)`. auto_init=True prints youtube-dl version? With quiet=True, `self.print_header`... Let me think: auto_init calls `self.print_header()` if not quiet... Actually:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True):\n        ...\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n```\n\nand print_header checks `self.params.get('quiet')`. With quiet=True, nothing printed. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, to be extra safe, I could avoid YoutubeDL entirely and use a minimal fake ydl object with the needed methods: urlopen, to_screen, report_warning, report_error, format_retries... But FileDownloader methods delegate: to_screen \u2192 self.ydl.to_screen(...); report_warning \u2192 self.ydl.report_warning; report_error \u2192 self.ydl.report_error; trouble \u2192 ... Since I monkeypatch dl.report_error and dl.report_warning in the tests that trigger them, and to_screen is used in real_download line 62 and report_retry_fragment/report_skip_fragment \u2014 those go to ydl.to_screen. A real YoutubeDL handles to_screen fine with quiet=True.\n\nHmm, but wait: FileDownloader.to_screen signature: `def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs)` \u2192 `self.ydl.to_screen(self.__msg(...)...)`? Let me think about what FileDownloader.to_screen does:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually in youtube-dl's common.py:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nI believe it just delegates. And YoutubeDL.to_screen with quiet=True does nothing. OK, real YoutubeDL is fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's a risk: `YoutubeDL.to_screen(self, msg, skip_eol=False)` checks `if self.params.get('logger')... else: ... if not self.params.get('quiet')...`. Fine.\n\nAlso `report_progress` hook added in FileDownloader.__init__ \u2014 it's only invoked via _hook_progress which we don't trigger (we patched fragment methods). Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 one more important check: in `make_downloader`, I do `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)`. But HlsFD calls `self.ydl.urlopen(...)`. Since dl.ydl is the same ydl object, the patched method is used. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)` sets an instance attribute shadowing the class method. monkeypatch handles undo. Good.\n\n### Assertion on `requested` in test 1: `requested == [MANIFEST_URL]` \u2014 _prepare_url returns url string when no http_headers. Good.\n\nBut in tests where info_dict has http_headers... none of my tests set http_headers. OK.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double check `_prepare_url`: \"returns sanitized_Request(url, None, headers) if headers else url\". info_dict.get('http_headers') \u2014 none of my info_dicts have it \u2192 returns url string. Good.\n\n### Now, the `test` param and `_download_fragment` results in test mode: test=True \u2192 break after first fragment. My results list [(True, b'frag0')] \u2014 fine.\n\n### Edge: In test 10 (retry/skip), after skipping frag0, `i += 1; media_sequence += 1` (lines 164-165) then `continue`. Then seg1 downloads. Good.\n\n### Check line 135: `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']: continue` \u2014 test 6 with fragment_index=1: frag0 \u2192 frag_index=1 <= 1 \u2192 skip; frag1 \u2192 frag_index=2 > 1 \u2192 download. Good.\n\n### Now let me also double check `patch_fragment_methods` fake_download signature: real code calls `self._download_fragment(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers)` \u2014 positional. My fake: `def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None)`. Good.\n\n### `fake_prepare_and_start(ctx)` \u2014 called as `self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`. Since I set instance attribute, `self` is not passed (instance attribute is a plain function, not bound). Wait \u2014 careful! If I do `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)`, then `dl._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` calls fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) \u2014 no self, because instance attribute lookup finds the plain function and calls it directly (no descriptor binding for instance attributes). Correct \u2014 plain functions stored as instance attributes are NOT bound. So fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) receives ctx only. Good, my signatures match (no self).\n\n### AES patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)` \u2014 module attribute. The code references `AES.new(...)` \u2014 module global lookup \u2192 FakeAES. Good.\n\n### FFmpeg patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)` \u2014 real_download references FFmpegFD global. Good.\n\n### Now \u2014 the `dl.report_error` / `dl.report_warning` monkeypatch: `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', recorder)`. FileDownloader.report_error is a bound method on the instance; setting instance attribute shadows it. Good.\n\nBut in tests where report_error is NOT patched (e.g., test 12 fragment failure) \u2014 no error reporting happens there (return False without report). Actually line 168-169 report_error is called in test 11 \u2014 I patch it there. In test 12, no report_error call. OK.\n\nWait, test 11: I patch report_error? The error message 'giving up after %s fragment retries' is reported. I should patch and assert. Let me add errors recording to test 11.\n\n### Let me also verify: in test 2/3, `self.report_error('pycrypto not found. Please install it.')` \u2014 patched recorder. Good.\n\n### Now, about `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` construction with hdrs={} \u2014 in Python 3, HTTPError.__init__(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp) \u2192 urllib.response.addinfourl? Actually HTTPError extends URLError and addinfourl... Let me recall:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n            ...\n        else:\n            self.headers = hdrs  # ?\n```\n\nHmm, in py3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self._method = None\n            addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in newer py3:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n       