est suite (they use FakeYDL actually). youtube-dl's own tests use:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL(YoutubeDL):\n    def __init__(self, override=None):\n        params = {'logger': FakeLogger(), ...}\n```\n\nHmm. I'll go with real YoutubeDL + quiet=True + monkeypatched urlopen. It's simple and robust.\n\nActually wait, one more consideration: `YoutubeDL({'quiet': True})` \u2014 the `params` dict is stored as self.params. HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params) \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__ sets self.params = params. Then `self.params.get('fragment_retries', 0)` etc. read from the same dict. When I pass params to make_downloader, I create YoutubeDL(params) and pass ydl.params. Good.\n\nHmm, but YoutubeDL.__init__ may add default params to the dict? It does `self.params = params` mostly, maybe sets some defaults like 'outtmpl'... Actually YoutubeDL.__init__ does:\n\n```python\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n        if 'outtmpl' not in self.params: ...\n```\n\nHmm, it might mutate. Doesn't matter \u2014 extra keys are harmless; 'test', 'fragment_retries', 'skip_unavailable_fragments' remain as set.\n\nWait, actually there's one potential gotcha: YoutubeDL.__init__ with auto_init might call `self.add_default_info_extractors()` etc.? No \u2014 auto_init does print_header, and... let me recall:\n\n```python\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n            # Set default...\n```\n\nActually in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True):\n        \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object...\"\"\"\n        if params is None:\n            params = {}\n        self._ies = []\n        self._pps = []\n        self._progress_hooks = []\n        self._postprocessors = []\n        ...\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n```\n\nHmm, and print_header:\n\n```python\n    def print_header(self):\n        if not self.params.get('quiet'):\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually I think auto_init also does `self._setup_opener()`? In newer youtube-dl, __init__ sets up the opener regardless. That involves building an opener object \u2014 harmless, no network.\n\nOK. Real YoutubeDL it is.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. There's one more thing: `YoutubeDL.to_screen` with quiet=True \u2014 but `report_retry_fragment` calls `self.to_screen(...)` on the *downloader* (FileDownloader.to_screen) which delegates to ydl.to_screen. With quiet=True, suppressed. Fine.\n\n### Let me also double-check `HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params)` \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params) \u2192 self.params = params. Good.\n\n### Now assemble the full test file. Also add a module-level docstring and keep top-level code minimal (only imports, helpers, tests).\n\nOne more coverage check \u2014 line 144 `headers = info_dict.get('http_headers', {})`: covered (no http_headers \u2192 {}). Should I also cover the case where http_headers exists? Line 144 is covered either way (the .get call executes). The branch `if byte_range:` both ways covered (test 8 true, others false). OK.\n\nLine 139 `re.match(r'^https?://', line)` \u2014 both branches covered (test 4: relative + absolute).\n\nLine 141 `if extra_query:` \u2014 both covered (test 7 true, others false).\n\nLine 145 `if byte_range:` \u2014 both covered.\n\nLine 147-153: while loop with success \u2192 break (covered), HTTPError (covered), not success \u2192 return False (covered).\n\nLine 160 `if count <= fragment_retries:` \u2014 in test 10: count goes 1 (<=1 \u2192 report retry), then 2 (>1 \u2192 no report). Both branches covered. In test 11 same.\n\nLine 162 `if count > fragment_retries:` \u2014 both covered (test 10: False for frag1... wait, for frag1 in test 10, count=0 after success \u2192 0 > 1 False \u2192 proceed. For frag0, count=2 > 1 \u2192 True \u2192 skip. Both covered.)\n\nLine 163 `if skip_unavailable_fragments:` \u2014 True in test 10, False in test 11. Covered.\n\nLine 171 `if decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128':` \u2014 both covered (test 4 false; tests 13-16 true).\n\nLine 172 `iv = decrypt_info.get('IV') or ...` \u2014 IV present (test 13), absent (test 14). Covered.\n\nLine 173 `decrypt_info['KEY'] = decrypt_info.get('KEY') or ...` \u2014 KEY None \u2192 fetch (tests 13-16). The `or` short-circuit: KEY set \u2192 no fetch. Is there a case where KEY is already set? Only if two fragments share the same key URI without a new EXT-X-KEY line between... Actually KEY is only set at fetch time; if two fragments follow one EXT-X-KEY line, the second fragment's `decrypt_info.get('KEY')` is the fetched key \u2192 no second fetch. Let me add that to a test: manifest with one KEY line and two fragments \u2192 key fetched once, used twice. That covers the short-circuit branch of line 173. Let me extend test 13 or make a separate test. I'll make test 13 have two fragments sharing the key:\n\nActually, let me keep test 13 with one fragment (clean assertions on requested order) and add the key-caching aspect to test 16? No \u2014 test 16 is about key change. Let me add a small test:\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_is_cached_between_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16), (True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested.count('https://example.com/key.bin') == 1\n    assert len(aes_calls) == 2\n    assert all(c.key == b'K' * 16 for c in aes_calls)\n```\n\nGood \u2014 covers the `decrypt_info.get('KEY')` truthy branch.\n\nLine 178 `if not test:` \u2014 True in tests 13-16 (test param False). False branch: test=True with AES-128 \u2192 skip decryption. Add to test 5? Test 5 is plain manifest. Let me add an AES test-mode variant... Or modify test 5's manifest to include AES-128? Then decryption skipped, append gets raw content. Hmm, that mixes concerns but covers line 178's False branch. Let me create a dedicated test:\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_skips_decryption_in_test_mode(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params={'quiet': True, 'test': True})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'raw-frag')])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert aes_calls == []\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'raw-frag']\n```\n\nNote: in test mode with AES-128, the key is still fetched? Line 173-174 executes regardless of test (KEY fetch happens before the `if not test` check). So requested includes key URL. But since I don't provide a response for it... my fake_urlopen falls back to manifest for unknown URLs! That would return the manifest text as the key \u2014 fine for this test since AES is patched out (key unused... wait, no: line 173-174 executes `self.ydl.urlopen(...).read()` \u2192 returns manifest bytes \u2192 decrypt_info['KEY'] = manifest bytes. Then `if not test:` \u2192 False \u2192 no AES.new. So no error. But requested would include the key URL. Fine. Actually, to be clean, provide the key response anyway. I'll add responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16}.\n\nLine 183 `if test:` \u2014 True in test 5 (break), False elsewhere. Covered.\n\nLine 187-199: EXT-X-KEY branch \u2014 covered by tests 13-16. Line 190 `if decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128':` \u2014 False branch: a KEY line with METHOD=NONE. Add to some manifest? E.g., test 4 could include `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=N