urn bytes.\n\nLet me now also think: does `_download_fragment` in real code get patched? Yes. But wait - is `_download_fragment` maybe used with `ctx` and does the real one handle retries internally? Doesn't matter, we patch it.\n\nHmm, one thing: the real `_download_fragment` signature is `(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers)` and returns `(success, frag_content)`. Our fake matches.\n\nNow, HTTPError construction: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. In Python 3, HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp). fp can be None. `report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)` - what does it do with err? Probably `self.to_screen(...)` with err (str(err) or something). Let me check report_retry_fragment:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries):\n        self.to_screen(\n            '[download] Got error: %s. Retrying fragment %d (%d/%d)...'\n            % (err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries))\n```\n\nHmm, in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries):\n        self.to_screen(\n            '[download] Got error: %s. Retrying fragment %d (%d/%d)...'\n            % (err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries))\n```\n\nSomething like that. Fine with real YoutubeDL.\n\n`report_skip_fragment`:\n\n```python\n    def report_skip_fragment(self, frag_index):\n        self.to_screen('[download] Skipping fragment %d...' % frag_index)\n```\n\nFine.\n\nNow, HTTPError's str: `str(err)` works.\n\nLet me now think about the structure of the final test file:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Tests for youtube_dl.downloader.hls.HlsFD.real_download (lines 61-216).\"\"\"\n\nimport binascii\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl import YoutubeDL\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error, compat_struct_pack\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader import hls as hls_module\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.hls import HlsFD\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    def __init__(self, url, data):\n        self._url = url\n        self._data = data\n\n    def geturl(self):\n        return self._url\n\n    def read(self):\n        return self._data\n\n\nclass FakeFFmpegFD(object):\n    instances = []\n\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n        self.ydl = ydl\n        self.params = params\n        self.hooks = []\n        self.called = False\n        self.filename = None\n        self.info_dict = None\n        FakeFFmpegFD.instances.append(self)\n\n    def add_progress_hook(self, ph):\n        self.hooks.append(ph)\n\n    def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n        self.called = True\n        self.filename = filename\n        self.info_dict = info_dict\n        return True\n```\n\nHmm, using a class-level `instances` list is state pollution across tests. Better: create the fake class inside the test or use a fixture that resets. Or use monkeypatch with a factory. Let me make FakeFFmpegFD created per-test via a helper that returns a fresh class:\n\nActually simpler: define FakeFFmpegFD at module level but have the test create its own instance-tracking via closure:\n\n```python\ndef make_fake_ffmpeg(monkeypatch):\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeFFmpegFD(object):\n        def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n            self.ydl = ydl\n            self.params = params\n            self.hooks = []\n\n        def add_progress_hook(self, ph):\n            self.hooks.append(ph)\n\n        def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n            calls.append((filename, info_dict, self.hooks))\n            return True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)\n    return calls\n```\n\nGood.\n\nHelper to build the downloader:\n\n```python\ndef make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, responses=None, params=None):\n    ydl = YoutubeDL(params or {'quiet': True})\n    responses = dict(responses or {})\n\n    def fake_urlopen(url):\n        url = url.get_full_url() if hasattr(url, 'get_full_url