ent` results in test mode: test=True \u2192 break after first fragment. My results list [(True, b'frag0')] \u2014 fine.\n\n### Edge: In test 10 (retry/skip), after skipping frag0, `i += 1; media_sequence += 1` (lines 164-165) then `continue`. Then seg1 downloads. Good.\n\n### Check line 135: `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']: continue` \u2014 test 6 with fragment_index=1: frag0 \u2192 frag_index=1 <= 1 \u2192 skip; frag1 \u2192 frag_index=2 > 1 \u2192 download. Good.\n\n### Now let me also double check `patch_fragment_methods` fake_download signature: real code calls `self._download_fragment(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers)` \u2014 positional. My fake: `def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None)`. Good.\n\n### `fake_prepare_and_start(ctx)` \u2014 called as `self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`. Since I set instance attribute, `self` is not passed (instance attribute is a plain function, not bound). Wait \u2014 careful! If I do `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)`, then `dl._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` calls fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) \u2014 no self, because instance attribute lookup finds the plain function and calls it directly (no descriptor binding for instance attributes). Correct \u2014 plain functions stored as instance attributes are NOT bound. So fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) receives ctx only. Good, my signatures match (no self).\n\n### AES patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)` \u2014 module attribute. The code references `AES.new(...)` \u2014 module global lookup \u2192 FakeAES. Good.\n\n### FFmpeg patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)` \u2014 real_download references FFmpegFD global. Good.\n\n### Now \u2014 the `dl.report_error` / `dl.report_warning` monkeypatch: `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', recorder)`. FileDownloader.report_error is a bound method on the instance; setting instance attribute shadows it. Good.\n\nBut in tests where report_error is NOT patched (e.g., test 12 fragment failure) \u2014 no error reporting happens there (return False without report). Actually line 168-169 report_error is called in test 11 \u2014 I patch it there. In test 12, no report_error call. OK.\n\nWait, test 11: I patch report_error? The error message 'giving up after %s fragment retries' is reported. I should patch and assert. Let me add errors recording to test 11.\n\n### Let me also verify: in test 2/3, `self.report_error('pycrypto not found. Please install it.')` \u2014 patched recorder. Good.\n\n### Now, about `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` construction with hdrs={} \u2014 in Python 3, HTTPError.__init__(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp) \u2192 urllib.response.addinfourl? Actually HTTPError extends URLError and addinfourl... Let me recall:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n            ...\n        else:\n            self.headers = hdrs  # ?\n```\n\nHmm, in py3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self._method = None\n            addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in newer py3:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n            self.fileno = ...\n```\n\nI recall HTTPError with fp=None works fine \u2014 e.g., `urllib.error.HTTPError('http://x', 404, 'Not Found', {}, None)` is commonly used in tests. Yes, that works. str(err) \u2192 'HTTP Error 404: Not Found'. Good.\n\n### error_to_compat_str(err) \u2014 youtube_dl.utils.error_to_compat_str \u2014 handles exceptions; str works.\n\n### format_retries(1) \u2014 FileDownloader.format_retries static: returns 'INF' if retries < 0 else str(retries)? Something like `return 'INF' if retries < 0 else str(retries)`. Fine.\n\n### Now \u2014 one thing I should double check: `YoutubeDL` import and instantiation cost. It's fine.\n\nAlternative: to minimize dependencies on YoutubeDL internals, I could build a tiny fake ydl:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL(object):\n    def __init__(self, params=None):\n        self.params = params or {}\n    def urlopen(self, url): ...\n    def to_screen(self, *a, **k): pass\n    def report_warning(self, *a, **k): pass\n    def report_error(self, *a, **k): pass\n```\n\nBut FileDownloader.to_screen delegates to ydl.to_screen \u2014 fine. report_warning \u2192 ydl.report_warning \u2014 fine. report_error \u2192 ydl.report_error \u2014 fine. But wait, does FileDownloader.report_error do more? `def report_error(self, *args, **kargs): self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)`? Hmm, actually in common.py:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure FileDownloader has report_error; it might. The get_info output for FileDownloader shows `report_error` exists. Good.\n\nUsing a real YoutubeDL is more faithful; using a fake is more hermetic. The real YoutubeDL with quiet=True is standard practice in youtube-dl's own test suite (they use FakeYDL actually). youtube-dl's own tests use:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL(YoutubeDL):\n    def __init__(self, override=None):\n        params = {'logger': FakeLogger(), ...}\n```\n\nHmm. I'll go with real YoutubeDL + quiet=True + monkeypatched urlopen. It's simple and robust.\n\nActually wait, one more consideration: `YoutubeDL({'quiet': True})` \u2014 the `params` dict is stored as self.params. HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params) \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__ sets self.params = params. Then `self.params.get('fragment_retries', 0)` etc. read from the same dict. When I pass params to make_downloader, I create YoutubeDL(params) and pass ydl.params. Good.\n\nHmm, but YoutubeDL.__init__ may add default params to the dict? It does `self.params = params` mostly, maybe sets some defaults like 'outtmpl'... Actually YoutubeDL.__init__ does:\n\n```python\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n        if 'outtmpl' not in self.params: ...\n```\n\nHmm, it might mutate. Doesn't matter \u2014 extra keys are harmless; 'test', 'fragment_retries', 'skip_unavailable_fragments' remain as set.\n\nWait, actually there's one potential gotcha: YoutubeDL.__init__ with auto_init might call `self.add_default_info_extractors()` etc.? No \u2014 auto_init does print_header, and... let me recall:\n\n```python\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n            # Set default...\n```\n\nActually in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True):\n        \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object...\"\"\"\n        if params is None:\n            params = {}\n        self._ies = []\n        self._pps = []\n        self._progress_hooks = []\n        self._postprocessors = []\n        ...\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n```\n\nHmm, and print_header:\n\n```python\n    def print_header(self):\n        if not self.params.get('quiet'):\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually I think auto_init also does `self._setup_opener()`? In newer youtube-dl, __init__ sets up the opener regardless. That involves building an opener object \u2014 harmless, no network.\n\nOK. Real YoutubeDL it is.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. There's one more thing: `YoutubeDL.to_screen` with quiet=True \u2014 but `report_retry_fragment` calls `self.to_screen(...)` on the *downloader* (FileDownloader.to_screen) which delegates to ydl.to_screen. With quiet=True, suppressed. Fine.\n\n### Let me also double-check `HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params)` \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params) \u2192 self.params = params. Good.\n\n### Now assemble the full test file. Also add a module-level docstring and keep top-level code minimal (only imports, helpers, tests).\n\nOne more coverage check \u2014 line 144 `headers = info_dict.get('http_headers', {})`: covered (no http_headers \u2192 {}). Should I also cover the case where http_headers exists? Line 144 is covered either way (the .get call executes). The branch `if byte_range:` both ways covered (test 8 true, others false). OK.\n\nLine 139 `re.match(r'^https?://', line)` \u2014 both branches covered (test 4: relative + absolute).\n\nLine 141 `if extra_query:` \u2014 both covered (test 7 true, others false).\n\nLine 145 `if byte_range:` \u2014 both covered.\n\nLine 147-153: while loop with success \u2192 break (covered), HTTPError (covered), not success \u2192 return False (covered).\n\nLine 160 `if count <= fragment_retries:` \u2014 in test 10: count goes 1 (<=1 \u2192 report retry), then 2 (>1 \u2192 no report). Both branches covered. In test 11 same.\n\nLine 162 `if count > fragment_retries:` \u2014 both covered (test 10: False for frag1... wait, for frag1 in test 10, count=0 after success \u2192 0 > 1 False \u2192 proceed. For frag0, count=2 > 1 \u2192 True \u2192 skip. Both covered.)\n\nLine 163 `if skip_unavailable_fragments:` \u2014 True in test 10, False in test 11. Covered.\n\nLine 171 `if decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128':` \u2014