        "reasoning": "Let me now write the complete test file based on what I know.\n\nKey facts gathered:\n- `HlsFD.can_download(manifest, info_dict)`:\n  - UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES = ('#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)', '#EXT-X-MAP:')\n  - is_aes128_enc = '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128' in manifest\n  - checks: no unsupported features, can_decrypt_frag or not aes128, not (aes128 and BYTERANGE), not info_dict.get('is_live')\n- `FragmentFD._prepare_url(info_dict, url)`: returns sanitized_Request(url, None, headers) if info_dict has http_headers else url.\n- `FragmentFD._download_fragment(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None)` returns (success, content).\n- `FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` calls _prepare_frag_download + _start_frag_download.\n- `FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, retries)` uses to_screen, error_to_compat_str, format_retries.\n- `FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment(frag_index)` uses to_screen.\n- `can_decrypt_frag = True` (module-level in hls.py presumably).\n- `parse_m3u8_attributes` parses KEY=value pairs.\n- FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params): self.ydl = ydl, self._progress_hooks = [], self.params = params, add_progress_hook(self.report_progress).\n\nImportant: FileDownloader.__init__ calls self.add_progress_hook(self.report_progress). So _progress_hooks contains report_progress. In the FFmpeg delegation path, `for ph in self._progress_hooks: fd.add_progress_hook(ph)` \u2014 my fake FFmpegFD must accept hooks. Fine.\n\nNow, since can_decrypt_frag is True (Crypto installed), AES-128 manifests pass can_download (as long as no BYTERANGE with AES-128, no unsupported methods, not live).\n\nManifests that pass can_download:\n- Plain: `#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n` \u2014 no #EXT-X-KEY, no #EXT-X-MAP \u2192 passes.\n- AES-128: `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"...\"` \u2192 passes (can_decrypt_frag True), but must NOT contain #EXT-X-BYTERANGE.\n- Unsupported: `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=SAMPLE-AES` or `#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"` \u2192 fails can_download.\n\nNow write the tests. Structure:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Coverage tests for youtube_dl.downloader.hls.HlsFD.real_download (lines 61-216).\"\"\"\n\nimport binascii\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl import YoutubeDL\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error, compat_struct_pack\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader import hls as hls_module\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.hls import HlsFD\n\nMANIFEST_URL = 'https://example.com/video.m3u8'\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    def __init__(self, url, data):\n        self._url = url\n        self._data = data\n\n    def geturl(self):\n        return self._url\n\n    def read(self):\n        return self._data\n\n\ndef _url_string(url):\n    if hasattr(url, 'get_full_url'):\n        return url.get_full_url()\n    return url\n\n\ndef make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, responses=None, params=None):\n    \"\"\"Build an HlsFD whose ydl.urlopen serves *manifest* (and extra responses).\"\"\"\n    ydl = YoutubeDL(params if params is not None else {'quiet': True})\n    responses = dict(responses or {})\n    requested = []\n\n    def fake_urlopen(url):\n        url = _url_string(url)\n        requested.append(url)\n        if url in responses:\n            result = responses[url]\n        else:\n            result = manifest\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return FakeResponse(url, result)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)\n    return HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params), requested\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)` \u2014 ydl is a local object; setattr on instance works with monkeypatch (it records and undoes, fine).\n\nHmm, one issue: `YoutubeDL(params)` \u2014 the real YoutubeDL __init__ with {'quiet': True}. It should be fine. But it may print stuff? With quiet=True, to_screen is suppressed. report_error raises YoutubeDLError? Let me think: YoutubeDL.report_error:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        self.trouble(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nand trouble:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        if self.params.get('logger'):\n            ...\n        else:\n            self.to_stderr(message)\n        if not expected:\n            self.params.get('logger')...\n        if self.params.get('raise_errors'...):\n            raise YoutubeDLError(message)\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        \"\"\"Report an error or warning...\"\"\"\n        if message:\n            if self.params.get('logger'):\n                self.params['logger'].error(message)\n            else:\n                self.to_stderr(message)\n        if self.params.get('raise_errors'):\n            raise YoutubeDLError(message)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure about raise_errors default. In youtube-dl, `raise_errors` defaults to False, so report_error just prints to stderr and sets... Actually there's also `self._download_retcode = 1` somewhere? In trouble:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        if message:\n            ...\n        if not expected:\n            self._download_retcode = 1\n```\n\nHmm, something like that. Either way, it won't raise by default. To be safe and to *assert* the error was reported, I'll monkeypatch `ydl.report_error` to record. Actually, better: monkeypatch the downloader's report_error? The HlsFD calls `self.report_error(...)` which is FileDownloader.report_error \u2192 `self.ydl.report_error(...)`. I can monkeypatch `dl.report_error` directly on the instance to record calls. That's cleaner for assertions and avoids stderr noise.\n\nSimilarly `report_warning` \u2192 `self.ydl.report_warning`. I'll monkeypatch `dl.report_warning` to record.\n\nBut careful: FileDownloader.report_error signature: `def report_error(self, *args, **kargs)` delegating. If I replace instance attribute `dl.report_error` with a lambda taking *args, fine.\n\nNow the fragment-method patcher:\n\n```python\ndef patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, results, fragment_index=0):\n    \"\"\"Replace FragmentFD's fragment machinery with recording fakes.\n\n    *results* is a list of ``(success, content)`` tuples or Exception\n    instances; the last entry is repeated once the list is exhausted.\n    \"\"\"\n    calls = {'prepare': [], 'download': [], 'append': [], 'finish': []}\n    state = {'next': 0}\n\n    def fake_prepare_and_start(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n        calls['prepare'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None):\n        calls['download'].append({'url': frag_url, 'headers': dict(headers or {})})\n        idx = state['next']\n        state['next'] += 1\n        result = results[idx] if idx < len(results) else results[-1]\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return result\n\n    def fake_append(ctx, frag_content):\n        calls['append'].append(frag_content)\n\n    def fake_finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_append_fragment', fake_append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_finish_frag_download', fake_finish)\n    return calls\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `results[-1]` fails if results is empty. Guard: if not results, default (True, b''). I'll ensure tests always pass non-empty results.\n\nHmm, also `dict(headers or {})` \u2014 headers may be a dict; fine.\n\nAES patcher:\n\n```python\ndef patch_aes(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Replace the module-level AES with a recording fake.\"\"\"\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeCipher(object):\n        def __init__(self, key, mode, iv):\n            self.key = key\n            self.mode = mode\n            self.iv = iv\n            calls.append(self)\n\n        def decrypt(self, data):\n            return b'decrypted:' + data\n\n    class FakeAES(object):\n        MODE_CBC = 'MODE_CBC'\n\n        @staticmethod\n        def new(key, mode, iv):\n            return FakeCipher(key, mode, iv)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)\n    return calls\n```\n\nFFmpeg patcher:\n\n```python\ndef patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch):\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeFFmpegFD(object):\n        def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n            self.ydl = ydl\n            self.params = params\n            self.hooks = []\n\n        def add_progress_hook(self, ph):\n            self.hooks.append(ph)\n\n        def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n            calls.append({'filename': filename, 'info_dict': info_dict, 'hooks': list(self.hooks)})\n            return True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)\n    return calls\n```\n\nNow the tests:\n\n### 1. test_delegates_to_ffmpeg_when_features_unsupported\n\nManifest with `#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"` (unsupported). No extra params. Expect:\n- returns True (from fake ffmpeg)\n- warning reported\n- ffmpeg called with filename and info_dict\n- progress hooks forwarded (FileDownloader.__init__ added report_progress hook, so hooks list non-empty)\n\n```python\ndef test_delegates_to_ffmpeg_when_features_unsupported(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    warnings = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_warning', lambda *a, **k: warnings.append(a))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL]\n    assert len(warnings) == 1\n    assert 'ffmpeg' in warnings[0][0]\n    assert len(ffmpeg_calls) == 1\n    assert ffmpeg_calls[0]['filename'] == 'out.ts'\n    assert ffmpeg_calls[0]['info_dict']['url'] == MANIFEST_URL\n    assert len(ffmpeg_calls[0]['hooks']) == len(dl._progress_hooks)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `dl._progress_hooks` contains report_progress (added in __init__). The fake fd's hooks should have the same count. Good.\n\nHmm, but `dl.report_warning` \u2014 FileDownloader.report_warning(self, *args, **kargs) \u2192 self.ydl.report_warning. Replacing instance attr with lambda *a: fine.\n\nAlso note: `man_url = urlh.geturl()` \u2014 FakeResponse.geturl returns the url string passed. In make_downloader, FakeResponse(url, result) where url is the string form. So man_url = MANIFEST_URL. Good.\n\n### 2. test_reports_pycrypto_error_with_extra_param\n\nManifest unsupported + info_dict has extra_param_to_segment_url. Expect return False, error reported.\n\n```python\ndef test_reports_pycrypto_error_with_extra_param(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', lambda *a, **k: errors.append(a))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(errors) == 1\n    assert 'pycrypto' in errors[0][0]\n    assert ffmpeg_calls == []\n```\n\n### 3. test_reports_pycrypto_error_with_decryption_key_url\n\nSame but `_decryption_key_url`.\n\n### 4. test_basic_download_all_fragments\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-TARGETDURATION:10\n#EXTINF:10,\nseg0.ts\n#EXTINF:10,\nhttps://other.example.com/seg1.ts\n```\ninfo_dict: {'url': MANIFEST_URL}. params default. results: [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')].\n\nExpect:\n- result True\n- downloads: first url = urljoin('https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'seg0.ts') = 'https://example.com/seg0.ts'; second = 'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts'\n- headers empty dicts\n- appended contents in order\n- finish called once\n- prepare called once with total_frags=2, ad_frags=0\n\n```python\ndef test_basic_download_all_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-TARGETDURATION:10\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL]\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['ad_frags'] == 0\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts', 'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert all(d['headers'] == {} for d in calls['download'])\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0', b'frag1']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 urljoin('https://example.com/video.m3u8', 'seg0.ts') = 'https://example.com/seg0.ts'. Yes.\n\n### 5. test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment\n\nparams {'quiet': True, 'test': True}. Manifest with 2 fragments. results [(True, b'frag0')].\n\nExpect: result True, one download, one append, no finish? Wait \u2014 `if test: break` happens after _append_fragment, then loop ends, then `self._finish_frag_download(ctx)` is called (line 214) \u2014 yes, finish IS called after break. So finish called once. And only 1 download/append.\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params={'quiet': True, 'test': True})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 1\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\n### 6. test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments\n\nfragment_index=1, manifest with 2 fragments. Expect only seg1 downloaded (frag_index 2 > 1). Download url 'https://example.com/seg1.ts'.\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag1')], fragment_index=1)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == ['https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag1']\n```\n\n### 7. test_extra_param_to_segment_url_added_to_fragment_urls\n\ninfo_dict with extra_param_to_segment_url='token=abc&sig=xyz'. Manifest with 1 fragment 'seg0.ts'. update_url_query('https://example.com/seg0.ts', {'token': ['abc'], 'sig': ['xyz']}) \u2192 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc&sig=xyz'.\n\n```python\ndef test_extra_param_to_segment_url_added_to_fragment_urls(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc&sig=xyz',\n    })\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['download'][0]['url'] == 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc&sig=xyz'\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 update_url_query ordering: parse_qs preserves order in py3.7+ (dict ordered). 'token=abc&sig=xyz' \u2192 {'token': ['abc'], 'sig': ['xyz']} \u2192 query 'token=abc&sig=xyz'. Should be fine. To be safe, use a single param: 'token=abc' \u2192 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc'. Safer. I'll use one param.\n\n### 8. test_ad_fragments_are_skipped\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=ad\nseg-ad0.ts\n#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=master\nseg0.ts\n#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad\nseg-ad1.ts\n#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment\nseg1.ts\n```\n\nWait \u2014 is_ad_fragment_start: `s.startswith('#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO') and 'type=ad' in s or s.startswith('#UPLYNK-SEGMENT') and s.endswith(',ad')`. Note operator precedence: (A and B) or (C and D). '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad' startswith '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT' and endswith ',ad' \u2192 True. Good.\n\nis_ad_fragment_end: '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO' and 'type=master' \u2192 True; '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment' \u2192 endswith ',segment' \u2192 True.\n\nSo counting loop: media_frags = 2 (seg0, seg1), ad_frags = 2. Download loop: ad fragments skipped via `if ad_frag_next: continue` (line 132-133), media fragments downloaded.\n\nExpect: prepare total_frags=2, ad_frags=2; downloads = [seg0, seg1] urls.\n\n```python\ndef test_ad_fragments_are_skipped(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=ad\\n'\n        'seg-ad0.ts\\n'\n        '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=master\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad\\n'\n        'seg-ad1.ts\\n'\n        '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['ad_frags'] == 2\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts', 'https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0', b'frag1']\n```\n\nWait \u2014 can_download on this manifest: no #EXT-X-KEY, no #EXT-X-MAP \u2192 passes. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad' \u2014 in the counting loop, is_ad_fragment_start('#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad') \u2192 True \u2192 ad_frag_next=True. Then 'seg-ad1.ts' \u2192 ad_frags += 1. Then '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment' \u2192 is_ad_fragment_start? startswith('#UPLYNK-SEGMENT') and endswith(',ad')? ',segment' doesn't end with ',ad' \u2192 False. is_ad_fragment_end \u2192 endswith(',segment') \u2192 True \u2192 ad_frag_next=False. Then 'seg1.ts' \u2192 media_frags += 1. \n\nIn the download loop: same logic with lines 209-212. Good.\n\n### 9. test_byterange_headers\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:1000@2000\nseg0.ts\n#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:500\nseg1.ts\n```\n\nFirst: sub_range_start=2000, byte_range={'start':2000,'end':3000}. Fragment 1: headers Range 'bytes=2000-2999'. Second: splitted_byte_range=['500'], len==1 \u2192 sub_range_start = byte_range['end'] = 3000; byte_range={'start':3000,'end':3500}. Fragment 2: Range 'bytes=3000-3499'.\n\ninfo_dict needs http_headers? Line 144: `headers = info_dict.get('http_headers', {})`. If absent, fresh {} each time, then Range added. Fine.\n\nExpect downloads with headers Range as above.\n\n```python\ndef test_byterange_sets_range_headers(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:1000@2000\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:500\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [d['headers'].get('Range') for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'bytes=2000-2999', 'bytes=3000-3499']\n```\n\n### 10. test_http_error_retries_then_skips\n\nparams: {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': True}. Manifest with 2 fragments. results: [HTTPError, HTTPError, (True, b'frag1')] \u2014 first fragment: attempt 0 raises (count\u21921), attempt 1 raises (count\u21922 > 1) \u2192 skip. Second fragment: (True, b'frag1').\n\nExpect: result True; downloads: seg0 twice, seg1 once; append only frag1.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retries_then_skips_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    params = {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': True}\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params=params)\n    error = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(MANIFEST_URL, 503, 'Service Unavailable', {}, None)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [error, error, (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [d['url'] for d in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts', 'https://example.com/seg0.ts',\n        'https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag1']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp): hdrs={} \u2014 in py3, HTTPError headers arg... `urllib.error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)` \u2014 hdrs should be an email.message or dict; dict works (it's passed to addinfourl... actually HTTPError.__init__ stores self.headers = hdrs). With fp=None, fine as long as nothing reads. report_retry_fragment does error_to_compat_str(err) \u2192 str(err) \u2192 'HTTP Error 503: Service Unavailable'. Fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` \u2014 in youtube_dl.compat, compat_urllib_error is the module urllib.error (py3). So HTTPError exists. Good.\n\n### 11. test_http_error_gives_up_when_skip_disabled\n\nparams: {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}. results: [error, error]. Expect result False, 2 downloads, no append, no finish.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_gives_up_when_skip_disabled(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    params = {'quiet': True, 'fragment_retries': 1, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params=params)\n    error = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(MANIFEST_URL, 500, 'Server Error', {}, None)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [error, error])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 2\n    assert calls['append'] == []\n    assert calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\n### 12. test_fragment_download_failure_aborts\n\nresults: [(False, None)]. Expect result False, no append, no finish.\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_download_failure_aborts(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(False, None)])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 1\n    assert calls['append'] == []\n    assert calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\n### 13. test_aes128_decryption_with_iv\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0X1234567890ABCDEF1234567890ABCDEF\nseg0.ts\n```\n\nWait \u2014 IV=0x... : `binascii.unhexlify(decrypt_info['IV'][2:].zfill(32))`. IV value '0X1234...' \u2192 [2:] = '1234...'. zfill(32) \u2014 the value is already 32 chars \u2192 unhexlify gives 16 bytes. Good. Use lowercase '0x' to be conventional.\n\nKey fetch: ydl.urlopen(_prepare_url(info_dict, 'https://example.com/key.bin')).read() \u2192 responses['https://example.com/key.bin'] = b'0123456789abcdef' (16 bytes).\n\nresults: [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)] \u2014 content 16 bytes (multiple of block size) \u2014 but I'm patching AES anyway, so content can be anything. Still, patch AES to assert key/iv.\n\nExpect:\n- result True\n- AES called once with key=b'0123456789abcdef', mode=FakeAES.MODE_CBC, iv=unhexlify('1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef')\n- append gets b'decrypted:' + content\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_decryption_with_iv(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    key = b'0123456789abcdef'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': key})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL, 'https://example.com/key.bin']\n    assert len(aes_calls) == 1\n    assert aes_calls[0].key == key\n    assert aes_calls[0].iv == binascii.unhexlify('1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef')\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00' * 16]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 requested: first urlopen for manifest, then key fetch. But note: the key fetch happens inside the fragment loop at line 173-174, i.e., during processing of seg0.ts. Order: manifest fetch, then key fetch. Yes.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, `_prepare_url(info_dict, 'https://example.com/key.bin')` \u2014 info_dict has no http_headers \u2192 returns the url string. Good.\n\n### 14. test_aes128_default_iv_uses_media_sequence\n\nManifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"key.bin\"\nseg0.ts\n```\n\n- MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5 \u2192 media_sequence = 5 (covers line 200-201)\n- KEY URI relative 'key.bin' \u2192 urljoin(man_url, 'key.bin') = 'https://example.com/key.bin' (covers 193-195)\n- No IV \u2192 iv = struct_pack('>8xq', 5) (covers 172)\n\nExpected iv: b'\\x00'*8 + struct.pack('>q', 5).\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_default_iv_uses_media_sequence_and_relative_key_uri(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert 'https://example.com/key.bin' in requested\n    assert aes_calls[0].iv == compat_struct_pack('>8xq', 5)\n    assert aes_calls[0].key == b'K' * 16\n```\n\nWait \u2014 compat_struct_pack \u2014 is it exported from youtube_dl.compat? The snippet imports `from youtube_dl.compat import ... compat_struct_pack`. Yes. compat_struct_pack = struct.pack.\n\nAlso note: media_sequence increments after each fragment: starts 5, after seg0 \u2192 6. Only one fragment, fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: `#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5` \u2014 line[22:] \u2014 '#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE' is 21 chars (let me count: #EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE = 1+4+1+1+5+1+8 = '#','E','X','T','-','X','-','M','E','D','I','A','-','S','E','Q','U','E','N','C','E' \u2192 21 chars). line[22:] = '5' (index 21 is ':'). Good.\n\n### 15. test_aes128_key_uri_updated_with_extra_query\n\ninfo_dict extra_param_to_segment_url='token=abc'. Manifest:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\nseg0.ts\n```\nKey URL after update: 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc'. responses must include that URL.\n\nExpect requested contains 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc'.\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_uri_gets_extra_query(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc' in requested\n```\n\n### 16. test_aes128_key_change_resets_cached_key\n\nManifest with two EXT-X-KEY lines with different URIs and two fragments:\n```\n#EXTM3U\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\nseg0.ts\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key2.bin\"\nseg1.ts\n```\n\nFlow: line EXT-X-KEY1: decrypt_url=None, new URI key1 \u2192 KEY=None (line 198-199 covered). Fragment seg0: KEY None \u2192 fetch key1. Line EXT-X-KEY2: decrypt_url='https://example.com/key1.bin' != 'https://example.com/key2.bin' \u2192 KEY=None (covered again). Fragment seg1: fetch key2.\n\nresponses: key1.bin \u2192 b'1'*16, key2.bin \u2192 b'2'*16.\n\nExpect: aes_calls[0].key == b'1'*16, aes_calls[1].key == b'2'*16. requested includes both key URLs.\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_change_resets_cached_key(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key2.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={\n            'https://example.com/key1.bin': b'1' * 16,\n            'https://example.com/key2.bin': b'2' * 16,\n        })\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16), (True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [c.key for c in aes_calls] == [b'1' * 16, b'2' * 16]\n    assert 'https://example.com/key1.bin' in requested\n    assert 'https://example.com/key2.bin' in requested\n```\n\n### 17. test_aes128_with_byterange_delegates_to_ffmpeg\n\ncan_download: `not (is_aes128_enc and '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE' in manifest)` \u2192 False when both. So manifest with AES-128 KEY + BYTERANGE \u2192 can_download False \u2192 no extra params \u2192 ffmpeg delegation. This covers the can_download interplay but lines are same as test 1. Optional; adds branch coverage of can_download but not of 61-216 beyond test 1. I could include it for robustness. Hmm \u2014 it does exercise line 68 with a different reason. I'll include it as it's cheap.\n\nActually, let me reconsider \u2014 the task says lines 61-216 don't execute; my tests cover them. Extra tests are fine but let me not bloat too much. I'll include it since it verifies a distinct branch of can_download interaction... Actually can_download is a static method outside 61-216. The delegation path (69-78) is already covered by tests 1-3. I'll skip test 17 to keep the file focused. Hmm, but it's a nice sanity check. I'll skip it \u2014 keep the suite tight.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider one more time whether I should also cover `is_live` info_dict \u2192 can_download False \u2192 delegation. Also outside 61-216. Skip.\n\n### Blank lines / comment lines coverage\n\nLines 93-94 (blank line continue), 95-100 (comment line, not ad marker \u2192 continue). My manifests have '#EXTM3U' (comment line \u2192 line 95 \u2192 not ad start/end \u2192 continue at 100) and trailing newline producing an empty last line? `'#EXTM3U\\n...\\nseg1.ts\\n'.splitlines()` \u2192 last element 'seg1.ts', no trailing empty. Let me add explicit blank lines in one manifest to cover 93-94. E.g., in test 4 manifest, add '\\n' lines. Actually splitlines on 'a\\n\\nb' \u2192 ['a', '', 'b'] \u2192 empty line \u2192 continue (93-94). Let me add a blank line in the basic manifest. I'll add one blank line in test_basic manifest.\n\nAlso line 130 `if line:` \u2014 the else branch (empty line) \u2192 falls through to next iteration... wait, line 129-130: `line = line.strip(); if line:` \u2014 if empty, nothing happens (no continue needed since rest of loop body is inside if). Covered by blank line too.\n\n### Now, double-check the counting loop vs download loop for '#EXTM3U'\n\n'#EXTM3U' startswith '#' \u2192 is_ad_fragment_start? No. is_ad_fragment_end? No. \u2192 continue. Good.\n\n### Check `update_url_query` import in hls module \u2014 used at line 142, 197. Fine.\n\n### One more check: test 7 (extra query) \u2014 can_download passes (no KEY/MAP). Good. And line 120-121 covered.\n\n### Check test 13 (AES with IV): can_download \u2014 is_aes128_enc True, can_decrypt_frag True \u2192 OK; no BYTERANGE \u2192 OK; is_live not set \u2192 OK. Passes. Good.\n\n### Potential issue: `YoutubeDL({'quiet': True})` \u2014 does YoutubeDL __init__ require anything? It sets up logger etc. Should be fine. But it might create no files. OK.\n\nActually, hmm, one concern: `YoutubeDL(params)` \u2014 in youtube-dl, `YoutubeDL.__init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True)`. auto_init=True prints youtube-dl version? With quiet=True, `self.print_header`... Let me think: auto_init calls `self.print_header()` if not quiet... Actually:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True):\n        ...\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n```\n\nand print_header checks `self.params.get('quiet')`. With quiet=True, nothing printed. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, to be extra safe, I could avoid YoutubeDL entirely and use a minimal fake ydl object with the needed methods: urlopen, to_screen, report_warning, report_error, format_retries... But FileDownloader methods delegate: to_screen \u2192 self.ydl.to_screen(...); report_warning \u2192 self.ydl.report_warning; report_error \u2192 self.ydl.report_error; trouble \u2192 ... Since I monkeypatch dl.report_error and dl.report_warning in the tests that trigger them, and to_screen is used in real_download line 62 and report_retry_fragment/report_skip_fragment \u2014 those go to ydl.to_screen. A real YoutubeDL handles to_screen fine with quiet=True.\n\nHmm, but wait: FileDownloader.to_screen signature: `def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs)` \u2192 `self.ydl.to_screen(self.__msg(...)...)`? Let me think about what FileDownloader.to_screen does:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually in youtube-dl's common.py:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nI believe it just delegates. And YoutubeDL.to_screen with quiet=True does nothing. OK, real YoutubeDL is fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's a risk: `YoutubeDL.to_screen(self, msg, skip_eol=False)` checks `if self.params.get('logger')... else: ... if not self.params.get('quiet')...`. Fine.\n\nAlso `report_progress` hook added in FileDownloader.__init__ \u2014 it's only invoked via _hook_progress which we don't trigger (we patched fragment methods). Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 one more important check: in `make_downloader`, I do `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)`. But HlsFD calls `self.ydl.urlopen(...)`. Since dl.ydl is the same ydl object, the patched method is used. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)` sets an instance attribute shadowing the class method. monkeypatch handles undo. Good.\n\n### Assertion on `requested` in test 1: `requested == [MANIFEST_URL]` \u2014 _prepare_url returns url string when no http_headers. Good.\n\nBut in tests where info_dict has http_headers... none of my tests set http_headers. OK.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double check `_prepare_url`: \"returns sanitized_Request(url, None, headers) if headers else url\". info_dict.get('http_headers') \u2014 none of my info_dicts have it \u2192 returns url string. Good.\n\n### Now, the `test` param and `_download_fragment` results in test mode: test=True \u2192 break after first fragment. My results list [(True, b'frag0')] \u2014 fine.\n\n### Edge: In test 10 (retry/skip), after skipping frag0, `i += 1; media_sequence += 1` (lines 164-165) then `continue`. Then seg1 downloads. Good.\n\n### Check line 135: `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']: continue` \u2014 test 6 with fragment_index=1: frag0 \u2192 frag_index=1 <= 1 \u2192 skip; frag1 \u2192 frag_index=2 > 1 \u2192 download. Good.\n\n### Now let me also double check `patch_fragment_methods` fake_download signature: real code calls `self._download_fragment(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers)` \u2014 positional. My fake: `def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None)`. Good.\n\n### `fake_prepare_and_start(ctx)` \u2014 called as `self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`. Since I set instance attribute, `self` is not passed (instance attribute is a plain function, not bound). Wait \u2014 careful! If I do `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)`, then `dl._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` calls fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) \u2014 no self, because instance attribute lookup finds the plain function and calls it directly (no descriptor binding for instance attributes). Correct \u2014 plain functions stored as instance attributes are NOT bound. So fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) receives ctx only. Good, my signatures match (no self).\n\n### AES patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)` \u2014 module attribute. The code references `AES.new(...)` \u2014 module global lookup \u2192 FakeAES. Good.\n\n### FFmpeg patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)` \u2014 real_download references FFmpegFD global. Good.\n\n### Now \u2014 the `dl.report_error` / `dl.report_warning` monkeypatch: `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', recorder)`. FileDownloader.report_error is a bound method on the instance; setting instance attribute shadows it. Good.\n\nBut in tests where report_error is NOT patched (e.g., test 12 fragment failure) \u2014 no error reporting happens there (return False without report). Actually line 168-169 report_error is called in test 11 \u2014 I patch it there. In test 12, no report_error call. OK.\n\nWait, test 11: I patch report_error? The error message 'giving up after %s fragment retries' is reported. I should patch and assert. Let me add errors recording to test 11.\n\n### Let me also verify: in test 2/3, `self.report_error('pycrypto not found. Please install it.')` \u2014 patched recorder. Good.\n\n### Now, about `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` construction with hdrs={} \u2014 in Python 3, HTTPError.__init__(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp) \u2192 urllib.response.addinfourl? Actually HTTPError extends URLError and addinfourl... Let me recall:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n            ...\n        else:\n            self.headers = hdrs  # ?\n```\n\nHmm, in py3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self._method = None\n            addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in newer py3:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n            self.fileno = ...\n```\n\nI recall HTTPError with fp=None works fine \u2014 e.g., `urllib.error.HTTPError('http://x', 404, 'Not Found', {}, None)` is commonly used in tests. Yes, that works. str(err) \u2192 'HTTP Error 404: Not Found'. Good.\n\n### error_to_compat_str(err) \u2014 youtube_dl.utils.error_to_compat_str \u2014 handles exceptions; str works.\n\n### format_retries(1) \u2014 FileDownloader.format_retries static: returns 'INF' if retries < 0 else str(retries)? Something like `return 'INF' if retries < 0 else str(retries)`. Fine.\n\n### Now \u2014 one thing I should double check: `YoutubeDL` import and instantiation cost. It's fine.\n\nAlternative: to minimize dependencies on YoutubeDL internals, I could build a tiny fake ydl:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL(object):\n    def __init__(self, params=None):\n        self.params = params or {}\n    def urlopen(self, url): ...\n    def to_screen(self, *a, **k): pass\n    def report_warning(self, *a, **k): pass\n    def report_error(self, *a, **k): pass\n```\n\nBut FileDownloader.to_screen delegates to ydl.to_screen \u2014 fine. report_warning \u2192 ydl.report_warning \u2014 fine. report_error \u2192 ydl.report_error \u2014 fine. But wait, does FileDownloader.report_error do more? `def report_error(self, *args, **kargs): self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)`? Hmm, actually in common.py:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure FileDownloader has report_error; it might. The get_info output for FileDownloader shows `report_error` exists. Good.\n\nUsing a real YoutubeDL is more faithful; using a fake is more hermetic. The real YoutubeDL with quiet=True is standard practice in youtube-dl's own test suite (they use FakeYDL actually). youtube-dl's own tests use:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL(YoutubeDL):\n    def __init__(self, override=None):\n        params = {'logger': FakeLogger(), ...}\n```\n\nHmm. I'll go with real YoutubeDL + quiet=True + monkeypatched urlopen. It's simple and robust.\n\nActually wait, one more consideration: `YoutubeDL({'quiet': True})` \u2014 the `params` dict is stored as self.params. HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params) \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__ sets self.params = params. Then `self.params.get('fragment_retries', 0)` etc. read from the same dict. When I pass params to make_downloader, I create YoutubeDL(params) and pass ydl.params. Good.\n\nHmm, but YoutubeDL.__init__ may add default params to the dict? It does `self.params = params` mostly, maybe sets some defaults like 'outtmpl'... Actually YoutubeDL.__init__ does:\n\n```python\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n        if 'outtmpl' not in self.params: ...\n```\n\nHmm, it might mutate. Doesn't matter \u2014 extra keys are harmless; 'test', 'fragment_retries', 'skip_unavailable_fragments' remain as set.\n\nWait, actually there's one potential gotcha: YoutubeDL.__init__ with auto_init might call `self.add_default_info_extractors()` etc.? No \u2014 auto_init does print_header, and... let me recall:\n\n```python\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n            # Set default...\n```\n\nActually in youtube-dl:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, params=None, auto_init=True):\n        \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object...\"\"\"\n        if params is None:\n            params = {}\n        self._ies = []\n        self._pps = []\n        self._progress_hooks = []\n        self._postprocessors = []\n        ...\n        self.params = params\n        ...\n        if auto_init:\n            self.print_header()\n```\n\nHmm, and print_header:\n\n```python\n    def print_header(self):\n        if not self.params.get('quiet'):\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually I think auto_init also does `self._setup_opener()`? In newer youtube-dl, __init__ sets up the opener regardless. That involves building an opener object \u2014 harmless, no network.\n\nOK. Real YoutubeDL it is.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. There's one more thing: `YoutubeDL.to_screen` with quiet=True \u2014 but `report_retry_fragment` calls `self.to_screen(...)` on the *downloader* (FileDownloader.to_screen) which delegates to ydl.to_screen. With quiet=True, suppressed. Fine.\n\n### Let me also double-check `HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params)` \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params) \u2192 self.params = params. Good.\n\n### Now assemble the full test file. Also add a module-level docstring and keep top-level code minimal (only imports, helpers, tests).\n\nOne more coverage check \u2014 line 144 `headers = info_dict.get('http_headers', {})`: covered (no http_headers \u2192 {}). Should I also cover the case where http_headers exists? Line 144 is covered either way (the .get call executes). The branch `if byte_range:` both ways covered (test 8 true, others false). OK.\n\nLine 139 `re.match(r'^https?://', line)` \u2014 both branches covered (test 4: relative + absolute).\n\nLine 141 `if extra_query:` \u2014 both covered (test 7 true, others false).\n\nLine 145 `if byte_range:` \u2014 both covered.\n\nLine 147-153: while loop with success \u2192 break (covered), HTTPError (covered), not success \u2192 return False (covered).\n\nLine 160 `if count <= fragment_retries:` \u2014 in test 10: count goes 1 (<=1 \u2192 report retry), then 2 (>1 \u2192 no report). Both branches covered. In test 11 same.\n\nLine 162 `if count > fragment_retries:` \u2014 both covered (test 10: False for frag1... wait, for frag1 in test 10, count=0 after success \u2192 0 > 1 False \u2192 proceed. For frag0, count=2 > 1 \u2192 True \u2192 skip. Both covered.)\n\nLine 163 `if skip_unavailable_fragments:` \u2014 True in test 10, False in test 11. Covered.\n\nLine 171 `if decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128':` \u2014 both covered (test 4 false; tests 13-16 true).\n\nLine 172 `iv = decrypt_info.get('IV') or ...` \u2014 IV present (test 13), absent (test 14). Covered.\n\nLine 173 `decrypt_info['KEY'] = decrypt_info.get('KEY') or ...` \u2014 KEY None \u2192 fetch (tests 13-16). The `or` short-circuit: KEY set \u2192 no fetch. Is there a case where KEY is already set? Only if two fragments share the same key URI without a new EXT-X-KEY line between... Actually KEY is only set at fetch time; if two fragments follow one EXT-X-KEY line, the second fragment's `decrypt_info.get('KEY')` is the fetched key \u2192 no second fetch. Let me add that to a test: manifest with one KEY line and two fragments \u2192 key fetched once, used twice. That covers the short-circuit branch of line 173. Let me extend test 13 or make a separate test. I'll make test 13 have two fragments sharing the key:\n\nActually, let me keep test 13 with one fragment (clean assertions on requested order) and add the key-caching aspect to test 16? No \u2014 test 16 is about key change. Let me add a small test:\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_is_cached_between_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16), (True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested.count('https://example.com/key.bin') == 1\n    assert len(aes_calls) == 2\n    assert all(c.key == b'K' * 16 for c in aes_calls)\n```\n\nGood \u2014 covers the `decrypt_info.get('KEY')` truthy branch.\n\nLine 178 `if not test:` \u2014 True in tests 13-16 (test param False). False branch: test=True with AES-128 \u2192 skip decryption. Add to test 5? Test 5 is plain manifest. Let me add an AES test-mode variant... Or modify test 5's manifest to include AES-128? Then decryption skipped, append gets raw content. Hmm, that mixes concerns but covers line 178's False branch. Let me create a dedicated test:\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_skips_decryption_in_test_mode(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params={'quiet': True, 'test': True})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'raw-frag')])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert aes_calls == []\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'raw-frag']\n```\n\nNote: in test mode with AES-128, the key is still fetched? Line 173-174 executes regardless of test (KEY fetch happens before the `if not test` check). So requested includes key URL. But since I don't provide a response for it... my fake_urlopen falls back to manifest for unknown URLs! That would return the manifest text as the key \u2014 fine for this test since AES is patched out (key unused... wait, no: line 173-174 executes `self.ydl.urlopen(...).read()` \u2192 returns manifest bytes \u2192 decrypt_info['KEY'] = manifest bytes. Then `if not test:` \u2192 False \u2192 no AES.new. So no error. But requested would include the key URL. Fine. Actually, to be clean, provide the key response anyway. I'll add responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16}.\n\nLine 183 `if test:` \u2014 True in test 5 (break), False elsewhere. Covered.\n\nLine 187-199: EXT-X-KEY branch \u2014 covered by tests 13-16. Line 190 `if decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128':` \u2014 False branch: a KEY line with METHOD=NONE. Add to some manifest? E.g., test 4 could include `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE` \u2014 wait, can_download: UNSUPPORTED_FEATURES '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)' \u2014 METHOD=NONE is allowed (negative lookahead matches non-NONE). So `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE` passes can_download and covers line 190 False branch (decrypt_info replaced with {'METHOD': 'NONE'}, no URI processing). Let me add that line to test 4's manifest. Wait, but careful: parse_m3u8_attributes('METHOD=NONE') \u2192 {'METHOD': 'NONE'}. Then line 190 check \u2192 'NONE' != 'AES-128' \u2192 skip. Then subsequent fragments: decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128' \u2192 False \u2192 no decryption. Good, test 4 has no encryption anyway. Adding `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE` to test 4 covers line 190's False branch. \n\nHmm wait, but does it affect anything else? decrypt_info becomes {'METHOD': 'NONE'} \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso line 191 `if 'IV' in decrypt_info:` \u2014 True (test 13), False (tests 14-16). Covered.\n\nLine 193 `if not re.match(r'^https?://', decrypt_info['URI']):` \u2014 True (test 14 relative), False (tests 13, 15, 16 absolute). Covered.\n\nLine 196 `if extra_query:` \u2014 True (test 15), False (13, 14, 16). Covered.\n\nLine 198 `if decrypt_url != decrypt_info['URI']:` \u2014 True (tests 13-16: first key line decrypt_url=None \u2260 URI). False branch: two consecutive EXT-X-KEY lines with the SAME URI \u2192 decrypt_url == URI \u2192 KEY not reset. Add to test 16? Let me add a same-URI duplicate line in test 16's manifest:\n\n```\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\n#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\n```\n\nSecond line: decrypt_url = 'https://example.com/key1.bin' == new URI \u2192 KEY stays None (it was None anyway). Hmm, that doesn't distinguish. To truly test the False branch meaningfully, I'd need KEY to be set... but KEY is only set during fragment download, and between two EXT-X-KEY lines there's no download. So the False branch just means KEY remains whatever it was (None initially). Coverage-wise, line 198 executes and the condition False \u2192 skip line 199. That's branch coverage of 198. Adding the duplicate line covers it. Let me add it to test 16's manifest. Actually wait \u2014 with the duplicate line, decrypt_url for line 2 = key1 URI, new URI = key1 URI \u2192 equal \u2192 line 199 skipped. Then line 3 (key2): decrypt_url = key1 \u2260 key2 \u2192 KEY=None. Good.\n\nHmm, but hold on: does adding the duplicate change the aes_calls assertions? No \u2014 key1 fetched once (cached), key2 fetched once. aes_calls keys: [b'1'*16, b'2'*16]. Good.\n\nLine 200-201 MEDIA-SEQUENCE \u2014 covered (test 14). False branch (line not present) \u2014 covered everywhere else.\n\nLine 202-208 BYTERANGE \u2014 covered (test 8). Both sub-branches of line 204 (len==2 vs len==1) covered.\n\nLine 209-212 ad markers in download loop \u2014 covered (test 8... I mean test 6/ad test).\n\nLine 214-216 \u2014 covered.\n\nLine 61-66 \u2014 covered everywhere.\n\nLine 68-78 \u2014 covered (tests 1-3 for False; tests 4+ for True).\n\nLine 69 `if info_dict.get('extra_param_to_segment_url') or info_dict.get('_decryption_key_url'):` \u2014 True (tests 2, 3), False (test 1). Covered.\n\nLine 80-86 \u2014 the nested function definitions execute when real_download runs (def statements). Covered.\n\nLine 88-104 \u2014 covered; line 101 `if ad_frag_next:` True (test 6), False (test 4). Line 96 True (test 6), 98 True (test 6). \n\nLine 93 `if not line: continue` \u2014 need a blank line in some manifest. Add to test 4.\n\nLine 112, 114-116, 122-127 \u2014 covered.\n\nOK now, let me also double check test 4's manifest with the METHOD=NONE line and blank line:\n\n```python\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-TARGETDURATION:10\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n```\n\ncan_download: no '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)' match (METHOD=NONE allowed), no '#EXT-X-MAP:' \u2192 passes. Good.\n\nWait \u2014 careful with the regex: '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)' \u2014 for the line '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE', after 'METHOD=' the lookahead (?!NONE|AES-128) fails since 'NONE' follows \u2192 no match. Good.\n\nHmm, one more: '#EXTINF:10,' lines start with '#' \u2192 comment branch. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 the counting loop counts media_frags: seg0, seg1 \u2192 2. Good.\n\n### Also \u2014 should I verify `ctx` contents passed to prepare? calls['prepare'][0] includes 'filename': 'out.ts', 'total_frags': 2, 'ad_frags': 0. I assert total_frags/ad_frags. Good.\n\n### Now, one concern about `patch_fragment_methods` and `dict(ctx)` in fake_prepare: ctx at that point has filename, total_frags, ad_frags. dict(ctx) copies. Good.\n\n### Another concern: in test 10, the HTTPError instance is reused for multiple raises \u2014 fine (exceptions can be raised multiple times).\n\n### Let me also think about whether `dl.real_download` could raise due to `self.to_screen` \u2014 FileDownloader.to_screen \u2192 ydl.to_screen \u2014 quiet \u2192 no-op. Good.\n\n### What about `report_progress` hook added in __init__ \u2014 `self.add_progress_hook(self.report_progress)` \u2014 just appends to list. Good.\n\n### Now write the final file. Also include a brief module docstring. Keep top-level code to imports + helper defs + test functions. No pytest.main.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should `make_downloader` use `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', ...)`? ydl is created inside the helper \u2014 monkeypatch fixture is passed in. Fine.\n\nOne more potential issue: `YoutubeDL` may emit warnings on init? No.\n\nAlso \u2014 `from youtube_dl.downloader import hls as hls_module` \u2014 importing hls imports FFmpegFD (external module) and Crypto. Should be fine in the test environment.\n\nLet me also double-check `compat_struct_pack` is importable from youtube_dl.compat \u2014 the snippet itself imports it from there. Good.\n\nNow, about assertion in test 14: `aes_calls[0].iv == compat_struct_pack('>8xq', 5)` \u2014 media_sequence at the time of seg0 download: MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5 set media_sequence=5; no increments before seg0. So iv = pack('>8xq', 5) = b'\\x00'*8 + b'\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x00\\x05'. Good.\n\nWait \u2014 order of lines: '#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5' comes before the KEY line and fragment. In the download loop, lines processed in order: MEDIA-SEQUENCE \u2192 media_sequence=5; EXT-X-KEY \u2192 decrypt_info set; seg0 \u2192 frag download, iv computed with media_sequence=5. Good.\n\n### Test 15 detail: extra_query applied to key URI: update_url_query('https://example.com/key.bin', {'token': ['abc']}) \u2192 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc'. The key fetch uses `info_dict.get('_decryption_key_url') or decrypt_info['URI']` \u2192 the updated URI. So responses key must be the updated URL. Good.\n\nAlso in test 15, the fragment URL also gets extra query: 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc'. I could assert that too. Let me assert both.\n\n### Test 13: `requested == [MANIFEST_URL, 'https://example.com/key.bin']` \u2014 order: manifest first, then key during fragment processing. Good.\n\n### Now, is there any issue with `FakeResponse.read()` being called multiple times? Only once per response. Fine.\n\n### Potential issue: `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_warning', ...)` \u2014 dl.report_warning is used at line 72. Recorder appends args. Assertion: warnings[0][0] contains 'ffmpeg'. The message: 'hlsnative has detected features it does not support, extraction will be delegated to ffmpeg'. Good.\n\n### Test 2/3: errors[0][0] == 'pycrypto not found. Please install it.' \u2014 assert 'pycrypto' in it.\n\n### Let me now also handle: in test 3, info_dict has '_decryption_key_url': 'https://example.com/decryption-key'. can_download doesn't look at info_dict except is_live \u2192 manifest unsupported \u2192 False \u2192 line 69 True \u2192 error. Good.\n\n### Final check on `patch_fragment_methods` results repeat-last semantics: test 11 results [error, error] \u2014 two attempts, both raise. count: attempt1 count=0\u21921 (<=1 \u2192 report retry), attempt2 count=1\u21922 (>1 \u2192 no report). Then count(2) > retries(1) \u2192 skip disabled \u2192 report_error \u2192 return False. Downloads recorded: 2. Good.\n\nWait, actually let me re-trace: `while count <= fragment_retries:` with fragment_retries=1: count=0 \u2192 try \u2192 raise \u2192 count=1; `if count <= fragment_retries:` 1<=1 \u2192 report_retry. Loop: count=1 <= 1 \u2192 try \u2192 raise \u2192 count=2; 2<=1 False \u2192 no report. Loop condition: count=2 <= 1 False \u2192 exit. `if count > fragment_retries:` 2>1 \u2192 skip_unavailable_fragments False \u2192 report_error \u2192 return False. Good: 2 download attempts.\n\nTest 10: fragment_retries=1, skip=True. frag0: 2 attempts (both raise) \u2192 count=2 > 1 \u2192 skip \u2192 i+=1, media_sequence+=1, report_skip_fragment, continue. frag1: attempt count=0 \u2192 success \u2192 break. Good: 3 download calls total.\n\n### One more: `results` for test 10: [error, error, (True, b'frag1')] \u2014 indices 0,1 for frag0 attempts; index 2 for frag1. Good.\n\n### Now, `calls['download']` entries: {'url', 'headers'}. In test 10, headers for each: {} (no byte_range). Fine.\n\n### Also assert in test 10 that report_skip_fragment was called? I'd need to patch it. Optional \u2014 the append assertion already proves the skip happened. I'll patch report_skip_fragment to record and assert, for good measure. Actually, keep it simpler: assert via downloads/appends. Hmm, but asserting report_skip_fragment exercises... it's called at line 166 \u2014 already covered. I'll add the patch anyway for a stronger postcondition. Let me add skip recording in test 10:\n\n```python\n    skips = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_skip_fragment', lambda *a: skips.append(a))\n    ...\n    assert skips == [(1,)]\n```\n\nWait, report_skip_fragment(self, frag_index) \u2014 instance attr lambda *a: a=(1,). Good.\n\nSimilarly report_retry_fragment in test 10: retries recorded [(err, 1, 1, 1)]. Let me record count values: `retries.append((a[1], a[2]))` \u2192 [(1, 1)]. Eh, let me just record all args and assert length 1. Keep it simple: assert len(retries) == 1.\n\nHmm, actually the retry report happens once (count=1 attempt). Good.\n\n### Also in test 11, patch report_error and assert message contains 'giving up after 1 fragment retries'.\n\nMessage: 'giving up after %s fragment retries' % fragment_retries \u2192 'giving up after 1 fragment retries'. Assert 'giving up after 1 fragment retries' in errors[0][0].\n\n### Now finalize. Also, should I include a test for `is_live` \u2192 delegation? Not needed for 61-216 (line 68 branch already covered both ways).\n\nLet me also double check that in test 1, `dl._progress_hooks` \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__ added report_progress \u2192 len 1. The fake ffmpeg records hooks list \u2192 len 1. Assert equality of lengths. Good.\n\n### Write the file now.\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Tests covering HlsFD.real_download (youtube_dl/downloader/hls.py, lines 61-216).\n\nAll network access and the FragmentFD bookkeeping machinery are replaced with\nrecording fakes (via ``monkeypatch``), so the tests are hermetic and leave no\nfiles or global state behind.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport binascii\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl import YoutubeDL\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_struct_pack, compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader import hls as hls_module\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.hls import HlsFD\n\nMANIFEST_URL = 'https://example.com/video.m3u8'\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal file-like object with geturl()/read(), like an urlopen result.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, url, data):\n        self._url = url\n        self._data = data\n\n    def geturl(self):\n        return self._url\n\n    def read(self):\n        return self._data\n\n\ndef _as_string(url):\n    return url.get_full_url() if hasattr(url, 'get_full_url') else url\n\n\ndef make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, responses=None, params=None):\n    \"\"\"Create an HlsFD whose ydl.urlopen serves *manifest*/*responses*.\"\"\"\n    ydl = YoutubeDL(params if params is not None else {'quiet': True})\n    responses = dict(responses or {})\n    requested = []\n\n    def fake_urlopen(url):\n        url = _as_string(url)\n        requested.append(url)\n        result = responses.get(url, manifest)\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return FakeResponse(url, result)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)\n    return HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params), requested\n\n\ndef patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, results, fragment_index=0):\n    \"\"\"Replace FragmentFD's fragment machinery with recording fakes.\n\n    *results* is a list of ``(success, content)`` tuples or Exception\n    instances; the last entry is repeated once the list is exhausted.\n    \"\"\"\n    calls = {'prepare': [], 'download': [], 'append': [], 'finish': []}\n    state = {'next': 0}\n\n    def fake_prepare_and_start(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n        calls['prepare'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None):\n        calls['download'].append({'url': frag_url, 'headers': dict(headers or {})})\n        index = state['next']\n        state['next'] += 1\n        result = results[index] if index < len(results) else results[-1]\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return result\n\n    def fake_append(ctx, frag_content):\n        calls['append'].append(frag_content)\n\n    def fake_finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_append_fragment', fake_append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_finish_frag_download', fake_finish)\n    return calls\n\n\ndef patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Replace the FFmpegFD used for the fallback path with a recording fake.\"\"\"\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeFFmpegFD(object):\n        def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n            self.ydl = ydl\n            self.params = params\n            self.hooks = []\n\n        def add_progress_hook(self, ph):\n            self.hooks.append(ph)\n\n        def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n            calls.append({\n                'filename': filename,\n                'info_dict': info_dict,\n                'hooks': list(self.hooks),\n            })\n            return True\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)\n    return calls\n\n\ndef patch_aes(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Replace the module level AES class with a recording fake.\"\"\"\n    calls = []\n\n    class FakeCipher(object):\n        def __init__(self, key, mode, iv):\n            self.key = key\n            self.mode = mode\n            self.iv = iv\n            calls.append(self)\n\n        def decrypt(self, data):\n            return b'decrypted:' + data\n\n    class FakeAES(object):\n        MODE_CBC = 'MODE_CBC'\n        new = staticmethod(lambda key, mode, iv: FakeCipher(key, mode, iv))\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)\n    return calls\n```\n\nHmm, `new = staticmethod(lambda ...)` inside class body \u2014 works. Or define a static method properly. Let me write it properly:\n\n```python\n    class FakeAES(object):\n        MODE_CBC = 'MODE_CBC'\n\n        @staticmethod\n        def new(key, mode, iv):\n            return FakeCipher(key, mode, iv)\n```\n\nFine.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_delegates_to_ffmpeg_when_features_unsupported(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    warnings = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_warning', lambda *args: warnings.append(args))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL]\n    assert len(warnings) == 1\n    assert 'delegated to ffmpeg' in warnings[0][0]\n    assert len(ffmpeg_calls) == 1\n    assert ffmpeg_calls[0]['filename'] == 'out.ts'\n    assert ffmpeg_calls[0]['info_dict']['url'] == MANIFEST_URL\n    assert len(ffmpeg_calls[0]['hooks']) == len(dl._progress_hooks)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `lambda *args: warnings.append(args)` \u2014 report_warning called as self.report_warning(msg) \u2192 since instance attr, called with (msg,) \u2192 args=(msg,). Good.\n\n```python\ndef test_reports_pycrypto_error_for_extra_param_to_segment_url(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', lambda *args: errors.append(args))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(errors) == 1\n    assert 'pycrypto not found' in errors[0][0]\n    assert ffmpeg_calls == []\n\n\ndef test_reports_pycrypto_error_for_decryption_key_url(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXT-X-MAP:URI=\"init.mp4\"\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', lambda *args: errors.append(args))\n    ffmpeg_calls = patch_ffmpeg(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        '_decryption_key_url': 'https://example.com/decryption.key',\n    })\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(errors) == 1\n    assert 'pycrypto not found' in errors[0][0]\n    assert ffmpeg_calls == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_basic_download_downloads_all_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-TARGETDURATION:10\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXTINF:10,\\n'\n        'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL]\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['filename'] == 'out.ts'\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['ad_frags'] == 0\n    assert [download['url'] for download in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts',\n        'https://other.example.com/seg1.ts',\n    ]\n    assert all(download['headers'] == {} for download in calls['download'])\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0', b'frag1']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, params={'quiet': True, 'test': True})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [download['url'] for download in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag1')], fragment_index=1)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [download['url'] for download in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg1.ts']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag1']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_extra_param_to_segment_url_is_added_to_fragment_urls(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['download'][0]['url'] == 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc'\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_ad_fragments_are_skipped(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=ad\\n'\n        'seg-ad0.ts\\n'\n        '#ANVATO-SEGMENT-INFO:type=master\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,ad\\n'\n        'seg-ad1.ts\\n'\n        '#UPLYNK-SEGMENT,segment\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['total_frags'] == 2\n    assert calls['prepare'][0]['ad_frags'] == 2\n    assert [download['url'] for download in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts',\n        'https://example.com/seg1.ts',\n    ]\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag0', b'frag1']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_byterange_sets_range_headers(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:1000@2000\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-BYTERANGE:500\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'frag0'), (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [download['headers'].get('Range') for download in calls['download']] == [\n        'bytes=2000-2999',\n        'bytes=3000-3499',\n    ]\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retries_then_skips_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg1.ts\\n'\n    params = {\n        'quiet': True,\n        'fragment_retries': 1,\n        'skip_unavailable_fragments': True,\n    }\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params=params)\n    error = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(\n        MANIFEST_URL, 503, 'Service Unavailable', {}, None)\n    retries = []\n    skips = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_retry_fragment', lambda *args: retries.append(args))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_skip_fragment', lambda *args: skips.append(args))\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [error, error, (True, b'frag1')])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [download['url'] for download in calls['download']] == [\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts',\n        'https://example.com/seg0.ts',\n        'https://example.com/seg1.ts',\n    ]\n    assert len(retries) == 1\n    assert skips == [(1,)]\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'frag1']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_gives_up_when_skipping_disabled(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    params = {\n        'quiet': True,\n        'fragment_retries': 1,\n        'skip_unavailable_fragments': False,\n    }\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest, params=params)\n    error = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(MANIFEST_URL, 500, 'Server Error', {}, None)\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', lambda *args: errors.append(args))\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [error, error])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 2\n    assert 'giving up after 1 fragment retries' in errors[0][0]\n    assert calls['append'] == []\n    assert calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_download_failure_aborts(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = '#EXTM3U\\n#EXTINF:10,\\nseg0.ts\\n'\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(monkeypatch, manifest)\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(False, None)])\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(calls['download']) == 1\n    assert calls['append'] == []\n    assert calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_decryption_with_explicit_iv(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,'\n        'URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",'\n        'IV=0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    key = b'0123456789abcdef'\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': key})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested == [MANIFEST_URL, 'https://example.com/key.bin']\n    assert len(aes_calls) == 1\n    assert aes_calls[0].key == key\n    assert aes_calls[0].iv == binascii.unhexlify('1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef')\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00' * 16]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 the manifest string concatenation: '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,' 'URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",' 'IV=0x...\\n' \u2014 adjacent string literals concatenate. The full line: '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef'. parse_m3u8_attributes regex: `(?P<key>[A-Z0-9-]+)=(?P<val>\"[^\"]+\"|[^\",]+)(?:,|$)`. For 'METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0x1234...': METHOD\u2192AES-128, URI\u2192\"https://example.com/key.bin\" (quoted, stripped), IV\u21920x1234... Good.\n\nHmm wait, one subtlety: the val regex `[^\",]+` for IV \u2014 '0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef' then end \u2192 matches with $ . Good.\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_default_iv_uses_media_sequence_and_relative_key_uri(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-MEDIA-SEQUENCE:5\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert 'https://example.com/key.bin' in requested\n    assert aes_calls[0].key == b'K' * 16\n    assert aes_calls[0].iv == compat_struct_pack('>8xq', 5)\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_uri_gets_extra_query(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {\n        'url': MANIFEST_URL,\n        'extra_param_to_segment_url': 'token=abc',\n    })\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert calls['download'][0]['url'] == 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc'\n    assert 'https://example.com/key.bin?token=abc' in requested\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_change_resets_cached_key(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key1.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key2.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={\n            'https://example.com/key1.bin': b'1' * 16,\n            'https://example.com/key2.bin': b'2' * 16,\n        })\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16), (True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [cipher.key for cipher in aes_calls] == [b'1' * 16, b'2' * 16]\n    assert requested.count('https://example.com/key1.bin') == 1\n    assert requested.count('https://example.com/key2.bin') == 1\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_key_is_cached_between_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n        'seg1.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, requested = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest, responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(\n        monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'\\x00' * 16), (True, b'\\x00' * 16)])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert requested.count('https://example.com/key.bin') == 1\n    assert len(aes_calls) == 2\n    assert all(cipher.key == b'K' * 16 for cipher in aes_calls)\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00' * 16] * 2\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_aes128_skips_decryption_in_test_mode(monkeypatch):\n    manifest = (\n        '#EXTM3U\\n'\n        '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\"\\n'\n        'seg0.ts\\n'\n    )\n    dl, _ = make_downloader(\n        monkeypatch, manifest,\n        responses={'https://example.com/key.bin': b'K' * 16},\n        params={'quiet': True, 'test': True})\n    calls = patch_fragment_methods(monkeypatch, dl, [(True, b'raw-frag')])\n    aes_calls = patch_aes(monkeypatch)\n\n    result = dl.real_download('out.ts', {'url': MANIFEST_URL})\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert aes_calls == []\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'raw-frag']\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nNow \u2014 wait, in test_aes128_skips_decryption_in_test_mode, the key IS fetched (line 173-174 runs before the test check). requested includes key URL \u2014 provided in responses. Good.\n\nHmm, one thing to double check in test_basic: the manifest contains '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE'. In the download loop, line 187: `elif line.startswith('#EXT-X-KEY'):` \u2192 decrypt_url = decrypt_info.get('URI') \u2192 None; decrypt_info = {'METHOD': 'NONE'}; line 190: 'NONE' != 'AES-128' \u2192 skip. Good. And subsequent fragments: decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128' \u2192 False \u2192 no decryption. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 potential problem: `#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=NONE` \u2014 can_download regex '#EXT-X-KEY:METHOD=(?!NONE|AES-128)' \u2014 no match. Good.\n\nAnother check \u2014 test_byterange: can_download \u2014 no KEY \u2192 is_aes128_enc False \u2192 checks pass. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration on `patch_fragment_methods` \u2014 `results[-1]` when index >= len(results). For test_basic, results has exactly 2 entries and 2 downloads \u2192 fine. For test 10, 3 entries, 3 downloads \u2192 fine. For test 11, 2 entries, 2 downloads \u2192 fine. OK, but the repeat-last fallback is there for safety.\n\nWait, there's an issue with test 11: results [error, error] \u2014 index 0 \u2192 error (raise), index 1 \u2192 error (raise). Good.\n\nNow \u2014 `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(MANIFEST_URL, 503, 'Service Unavailable', {}, None)` \u2014 in Python 3, HTTPError.__init__ with fp=None: \n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self._method = None  # ?\n```\n\nHmm, actually current CPython:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is None:\n            self._orig_fp = None\n        ...\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident `HTTPError(url, code, msg, {}, None)` works \u2014 it's used widely in tests. error_to_compat_str(err) \u2192 str(err) \u2192 'HTTP Error 503: Service Unavailable'. Good.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 error_to_compat_str: \n\n```python\ndef error_to_compat_str(exception):\n    error_str = str(exception)\n    ...\n    return error_str\n```\n\nFine.\n\nAnd `format_retries(1)` \u2192 '1'. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 one more thought about `YoutubeDL({'quiet': True})`: YoutubeDL.__init__ might require 'logger' handling etc. It's fine. But \u2014 hmm, does YoutubeDL.__init__ possibly call `self._setup_opener()` which creates a urllib opener \u2014 no network. Fine.\n\nActually, wait. There's one more potential gotcha: YoutubeDL.__init__ in youtube-dl does:\n\n```python\n        self.params['outtmpl'] = ...\n```\n\nNo... it does things like:\n\n```python\n        if params.get('bidi_workaround')...\n```\n\nNothing problematic with {'quiet': True}.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 I realize there might be an issue with `YoutubeDL.to_screen` when 'quiet' is True: it returns without printing. Good.\n\nBut `report_warning` \u2192 `self.ydl.report_warning(message)` \u2192 YoutubeDL.report_warning \u2192 `self.report_warning(message)`... In the delegation test I patch dl.report_warning (the downloader's), so ydl's isn't called. Good.\n\nIn tests where I don't patch report_warning/report_error but they might be called: \n- test_basic etc.: no warnings/errors expected. Actually wait \u2014 does anything call report_warning in the happy path? No.\n- test 10 (retry/skip): report_retry_fragment and report_skip_fragment are patched on dl. But wait \u2014 I patch `dl.report_retry_fragment` \u2014 the code calls `self.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)` \u2192 instance attr \u2192 recorder. Good. report_skip_fragment similarly.\n\nHmm, but hold on: FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment is defined on FragmentFD (per get_info). The code calls self.report_retry_fragment \u2014 instance attribute patch works.\n\nOK. Now \u2014 one more subtle thing: in `make_downloader`, `HlsFD(ydl, ydl.params)` \u2014 FileDownloader.__init__ does `self.add_progress_hook(self.report_progress)`. report_progress is a bound method; fine.\n\nNow let me reconsider the `test` param propagation: `self.params.get('test', False)` \u2014 params is the dict I passed. Good.\n\nAlso `fragment_retries` default 0: in tests without it, `while count <= 0:` \u2192 one attempt; on HTTPError \u2192 count=1; `if count <= 0` False; loop exits; count(1) > 0 \u2192 skip (default skip_unavailable_fragments True) \u2192 skip. Not tested but fine.\n\nNow \u2014 is there any issue with `calls['prepare'][0]['filename']`? ctx includes 'filename': filename \u2192 'out.ts'. Good.\n\nLet me also double-check `update_url_query` behavior for 'https://example.com/seg0.ts' with {'token': ['abc']}:\n\n```python\ndef update_url_query(url, query):\n    ...\n    parsed_url = compat_urlparse.urlparse(url)\n    ...\n    return compat_urlparse.urlunparse(parsed_url._replace(query=compat_urllib_parse_urlencode(...)))\n```\n\nResult: 'https://example.com/seg0.ts?token=abc'. Good.\n\nAnd parse_qs('token=abc') \u2192 {'token': ['abc']}. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing I want to double-check: in test_extra_param, can_download passes (no KEY/MAP in manifest). Good \u2014 extra_param_to_segment_url doesn't affect can_download.\n\nNow, the final concern: **does `HlsFD.real_download` reference `self._progress_hooks` before FFmpegFD construction?** Yes, lines 76-77. Fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 `FFmpegFD(self.ydl, self.params)` \u2014 my FakeFFmpegFD.__init__(ydl, params) matches positional call. Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', ...)` could interfere with `FragmentFD` class \u2014 no, instance-level only.\n\nOne more: the `state` dict in patch_fragment_methods \u2014 closure per test. Good.\n\nNow, about imports: `import binascii` used in test_aes128_decryption_with_explicit_iv. `pytest` imported but unused? I use monkeypatch fixture (no direct pytest reference needed). Unused import \u2192 flake but harmless. Actually, I don't need pytest import at all since I don't use pytest.raises or marks. Let me drop it... Actually keep it minimal: drop `import pytest`. Hmm, but no top-level code other than defs \u2014 fine without pytest.\n\nWait \u2014 do I use pytest anywhere? No. Drop it.\n\nDo I use `compat_struct_pack`? Yes in test 14. `compat_urllib_error`? Yes. `binascii`? Yes.\n\nLet me also double check `from youtube_dl.downloader import hls as hls_module` \u2014 hls.py imports FFmpegFD from .external \u2014 importing external imports ffmpeg stuff \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, one more re-examination of the AES-128 + test-mode interplay in test_aes128_skips_decryption_in_test_mode: line 171 `if decrypt_info['METHOD'] == 'AES-128':` \u2192 True; line 172 iv computed; line 173 KEY fetch (urlopen called \u2014 responses has key.bin \u2192 b'K'*16); line 178 `if not test