4 report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries) \u2192 args=(err, 1, 1, 1). len(retries)==1. Good.\n\nNow, assemble everything. Also add small docstrings to tests? Keep concise.\n\nOne more review of patch_aes: `AES.new(key, AES.MODE_CBC, iv)` \u2192 FakeAES.new(key, 'MODE_CBC', iv) \u2192 FakeCipher. calls appended. decrypt returns b'decrypted:' + data. In test 13, append == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00'*16]. Good.\n\nWait \u2014 in test 13, `calls['append'] == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00' * 16]` \u2014 the decrypted content. Good.\n\nAlso in test_aes128_key_is_cached_between_fragments: append == [b'decrypted:' + b'\\x00'*16] * 2. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more thought: could `parse_m3u8_attributes` choke on 'METHOD=AES-128,URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",IV=0x...'? The regex findall: \n- 'METHOD=AES-128,' \u2192 key=METHOD, val=AES-128 (matches [^\",]+ up to ','). \n- 'URI=\"https://example.com/key.bin\",' \u2192 val matches \"[^\"]+\" \u2192 quoted. \n- 'IV=0x1234...' \u2192 val [^\",]+ to $. \nGood.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, one subtle thing: the URI contains ':' and '/' \u2014 fine for [^\"]+.\n\nBut careful: 'https://example.com/key.bin' \u2014 the regex `(?P<key>[A-Z0-9-]+)=(?P<val>\"[^\"]+\"|[^\",]+)(?:,|$)` \u2014 for URI, val starts with '\"' \u2192 matches quoted form. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 the IV zfill: '0x1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef'[2:] = '1234567890abcdef1234567890abcdef' (32 chars) \u2192 zfill(32) no-op \u2192 unhexlify \u2192 16 bytes: b'\\x12\\x34\\x56\\x78\\x90\\xab\\xcd\\xef' * 2. Assertion compares to binascii.unhexlify of the same string. Good.\n\nNow, let me also think: do I need `@pytest.mark.parametrize` anywhere? No.\n\nFinal structure check \u2014 top-level code: imports, class FakeResponse, helper functions, test functions. No pytest.main. Good.\n\nWait, one more thing \u2014 the instruction says \"Write as little top-level code as possible\". Helpers are necessary; fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the te