al YoutubeDL handles to_screen fine with quiet=True.\n\nHmm, but wait: FileDownloader.to_screen signature: `def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs)` \u2192 `self.ydl.to_screen(self.__msg(...)...)`? Let me think about what FileDownloader.to_screen does:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually in youtube-dl's common.py:\n\n```python\n    def to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n        \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n        self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nI believe it just delegates. And YoutubeDL.to_screen with quiet=True does nothing. OK, real YoutubeDL is fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's a risk: `YoutubeDL.to_screen(self, msg, skip_eol=False)` checks `if self.params.get('logger')... else: ... if not self.params.get('quiet')...`. Fine.\n\nAlso `report_progress` hook added in FileDownloader.__init__ \u2014 it's only invoked via _hook_progress which we don't trigger (we patched fragment methods). Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 one more important check: in `make_downloader`, I do `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)`. But HlsFD calls `self.ydl.urlopen(...)`. Since dl.ydl is the same ydl object, the patched method is used. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(ydl, 'urlopen', fake_urlopen)` sets an instance attribute shadowing the class method. monkeypatch handles undo. Good.\n\n### Assertion on `requested` in test 1: `requested == [MANIFEST_URL]` \u2014 _prepare_url returns url string when no http_headers. Good.\n\nBut in tests where info_dict has http_headers... none of my tests set http_headers. OK.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double check `_prepare_url`: \"returns sanitized_Request(url, None, headers) if headers else url\". info_dict.get('http_headers') \u2014 none of my info_dicts have it \u2192 returns url string. Good.\n\n### Now, the `test` param and `_download_fragment` results in test mode: test=True \u2192 break after first fragment. My results list [(True, b'frag0')] \u2014 fine.\n\n### Edge: In test 10 (retry/skip), after skipping frag0, `i += 1; media_sequence += 1` (lines 164-165) then `continue`. Then seg1 downloads. Good.\n\n### Check line 135: `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']: continue` \u2014 test 6 with fragment_index=1: frag0 \u2192 frag_index=1 <= 1 \u2192 skip; frag1 \u2192 frag_index=2 > 1 \u2192 download. Good.\n\n### Now let me also double check `patch_fragment_methods` fake_download signature: real code calls `self._download_fragment(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers)` \u2014 positional. My fake: `def fake_download(ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None)`. Good.\n\n### `fake_prepare_and_start(ctx)` \u2014 called as `self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`. Since I set instance attribute, `self` is not passed (instance attribute is a plain function, not bound). Wait \u2014 careful! If I do `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare_and_start)`, then `dl._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` calls fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) \u2014 no self, because instance attribute lookup finds the plain function and calls it directly (no descriptor binding for instance attributes). Correct \u2014 plain functions stored as instance attributes are NOT bound. So fake_prepare_and_start(ctx) receives ctx only. Good, my signatures match (no self).\n\n### AES patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'AES', FakeAES)` \u2014 module attribute. The code references `AES.new(...)` \u2014 module global lookup \u2192 FakeAES. Good.\n\n### FFmpeg patch: `monkeypatch.setattr(hls_module, 'FFmpegFD', FakeFFmpegFD)` \u2014 real_download references FFmpegFD global. Good.\n\n### Now \u2014 the `dl.report_error` / `dl.report_warning` monkeypatch: `monkeypatch.setattr(dl, 'report_error', recorder)`. FileDownloader.report_error is a bound method on the instance; setting instance attribute shadows it. Good.\n\nBut in tests where report_error is NOT patched (e.g., test 12 fragment failure) \u2014 no error reporting happens there (return False without report). Actually line 168-169 report_error is called in test 11 \u2014 I patch it there. In test 12, no report_error call. OK.\n\nWait, test 11: I patch report_error? The error message 'giving up after %s fragment retries' is reported. I should patch and assert. Let me add errors recording to test 11.\n\n### Let me also verify: in test 2/3, `self.report_error('pycrypto not found. Please install it.')` \u2014 patched recorder. Good.\n\n### Now, about `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` construction with hdrs={} \u2014 in Python 3, HTTPError.__init__(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp) \u2192 urllib.response.addinfourl? Actually HTTPError extends URLError and addinfourl... Let me recall:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.headers = hdrs\n            ...\n        else:\n            self.headers = hdrs  # ?\n```\n\nHmm, in py3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        # The addinfourl that we've inherited from doesn't have a\n        # __init__ that takes the parameters we need...\n        if fp is not None:\n            self._method = None\n            addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in newer py3:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp